INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age', 'telangana-history-en', '') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = '';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p1", "partName": "Chalukyan Age — Overview", "bodyMd": "After the fall of the Satavahanas, several parts of South India came under the rule of the Chalukyas, whose age lasted for around six centuries. The branch that ruled from Badami (Vatapi) in Bijapur district formed the parental line — the Badami (Western) Chalukyas. The Eastern Chalukyas branched off from them and ruled Vengi uninterruptedly for four and a half centuries; towards the end, they became the descendants of the Tanjavoor Cholas and ruled the Tamil region for another two and a half centuries.\n\nUnder Chalukyan patronage, South Indian culture flourished both politically and culturally. Hindu religious culture, which had taken birth under the Ikshvakus and Vishnukundins, reached its blossoming under the Chalukyas. Vedanta literature, architecture and sculpture attained their final, highest form during this period, and the temples the Chalukyas built in Karnataka, Gujarat, Maharashtra and Andhradesa remain storehouses of Chalukyan-style art. Regional languages — Telugu, Kannada, Marathi and Gujarati — all attained new literary form during Chalukyan times.\n\n**The Chalukyan branches and their periods:**\n\n| Branch | Period | Core Territory |\n|---|---|---|\n| Badami (Western) Chalukyas | 543 – 752 A.D. | Maharashtra and Karnataka |\n| Eastern Chalukyas (Vengi Chalukyas) | 624 – 1075 A.D. | Vengi (Andhra) |\n| Vemulavada Chalukyas | 750 – 973 A.D. | Northwestern Telangana, as feudatories of the Rashtrakutas |\n| Mudigonda Chalukyas | 850 – 1200 A.D. | Areas around Khammam |\n| Kalyani Chalukyas | 973 – 1157 A.D. | Belonged to the Badami Chalukya line; ruled from Karnataka |\n\nEach of these branches contributed to the development of its respective region as a regional administrative centre, with its own specialities in art, literature and governance.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p2", "partName": "Sources of Chalukyan History", "bodyMd": "Like the history of other South Indian ruling dynasties, Chalukyan history depends mainly on stone and copper-plate inscriptions. Most information on the Eastern Chalukyas comes from copper-plate grants. In Chalukyan inscriptions, historians notice three distinct phases:\n\n1.  **Early phase (Kubja Vishnuvardhana to Vishnuvardhana V):** Inscriptions of this phase name only three generations — the donor, his father and grandfather — with no historically valuable information about victories or the sequence of rulers beyond who succeeded whom.\n2.  **Change after Gunaga Vijayaditya (Vijayaditya III):** From his time, inscriptions begin recording genealogy and events in more detail.\n3.  **Later detailed inscriptions**, which supply administrative and military information.\n\n**Key inscriptions and what they reveal:**\n\n| Inscription | Date | Significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sataluru grant of Gunaga Vijayaditya | — | Gives the chronology and rule periods of earlier Eastern Chalukya rulers |\n| Neelgund inscription | 866 A.D. | Records Gunaga Vijayaditya as a feudatory of Amoghavarsha I (Rashtrakuta), paying tribute to him |\n| Sanjan copper plate | 872 A.D. | Records that, unlike other feudatories, Gunaga Vijayaditya personally cleaned Amoghavarsha''s walking path |\n| Kopparam inscription | 631 A.D. | Pulakesi II refers to Vishnuvardhana as heir-apparent (Yuvaraja) |\n| Cheepurupalli inscription | 641 A.D. | Shows Vishnuvardhana had assumed the title of maharaja and ruled independently |\n| Aihole (Ayyavolu) prasasti | — | Composed in Sanskrit by Ravikeerthi, Pulakesi II''s War Minister; the main source for Pulakesi II''s reign |\n| Kollipara copper plate | — | Records Arikesari I''s victories and administration (Vemulavada Chalukyas); its authenticity has been questioned by some scholars |\n| Kuruvagattu stone inscription | — | Issued by Beeragruha (Mahaboobnagar), informs on Vemulavada administrative details |\n| Vemulavada copper plate of Arikesari II & Chennur copper plate | 941 A.D. | Record how Arikesari II sheltered Yuddhamalla I and made him ruler of Chennur |\n| Karimnagar copper plate | 946 A.D. | Traces Vemulavada developments from Yuddhamalla to Arikesari |\n| Kurkyala inscription | 940 A.D. | Written by Jayavallabha (brother of the poet Pampa); records that Pampa wrote _Adipurana_ in 941 A.D. |\n| Parbhani copper plate (Arikesari III) | 966 A.D. | Records that his Subahdar built Jain temples |\n| Repaka inscription (Arikesari) | 968 A.D. | Records construction of a Jain temple and land grants at Repaka |\n\n**Literary sources:** _Vikramarjuna Vijayam_, written in Kannada by the poet Pampa, is a prominent work of the Vemulavada Chalukya period; it contains a eulogy (prasasti) of Arikesari II and explains his relations with the Rashtrakutas. Somadevasuri, a Jain scholar, wrote _Yasastilaka Champukavyam_ (also called _Yasodhara Charitra_) during the time of the later Vemulavada kings — a key source for the religious doctrines of Jainism as well as social and political details of the age. Somadevasuri''s _Neetikavyamruta_ is also regarded as a leading literary source of the period.\n\nOnly two donors of copper-plate inscriptions are known from Mudigonda Chalukya times: the **Mogala Cheruvu inscription** of Kusumayudha IV, and the **Krivvaka inscription** of Kusumayudha VI (informs on chronology). The **Narayanagiri copper plate** (1004 A.D.) describes the administration of Chalukya Gonagarasa, who ruled the Warangal region. The **Bezawada copper plate** (892 A.D.) records that Kusumayudha donated Kukiparru village to a brahmin, Potamaiah. The **Koravi inscription** (935 A.D.) is another important source.\n\n**Coins as archaeological sources:** Gold coins bearing the name \"Chalukya Chandra,\" issued during the time of Eastern Chalukya Saktivarma (999–1011 A.D.), have been found in Arakan and Siam. Gold, silver and copper coins circulated during the Chalukyan age. The gold coin was called _gadya_ (weighing 88 grains), with variants such as _Rajanarayana gadya_, _Surabhi gadya_ and _Tyaga gadya_. The silver coin was called _mada_, and _kasu_ was the name used for the copper coin.\n\n**Monuments as sources:** Architectural and sculptural monuments testify to Chalukyan religious patronage across Jainism, Saivism and Vaishnavism, treated with equal favour. Since no temples existed anywhere in India for Brahma, the first of the trimurthis, the Western Chalukyas met this gap by constructing the Nava Brahma temples at Alampur. The Raja Rajeswara temple built by the Vemulavada Chalukyas at Vemulavada became famous as a Saiva temple of the age. Chalukyan architecture attained special significance at Alampur, and the Western Chalukyas also built famous structures at Badami and Pattadakal.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p3", "partName": "Origin of the Word \"Chalukya\"", "bodyMd": "No certain sources establish the origin of the Chalukya dynastic name or their place of origin; different inscriptions offer differing, partly legendary, accounts.\n\n**1\\. Chandravamsa/Ayodhya** **Traditions**\n\n*   Some inscriptions state Chalukyas belonged to the Chandravamsa that ruled Ayodhya and came south seeking victory, settling there over time. Modern historians give little weight to this, since Ayodhya was never the capital of Chandravamsa kings in later-attested history.\n*   In the **Solanki inscriptions** of Gujarat, it is stated that the dynasty''s founder was born from the _chalukya_ (palm) of Brahma — hence the name Chalukya.\n*   Bilhana''s _Vikramankadeva Charitra_ narrates a variant version of this same \"born from Brahma''s palm\" story: amid sufferings caused by rakshasas, Brahma, while offering oblation to the Sun after hearing Indra''s prayer, saw a great warrior born from his palm (_chuluka_) — armoured and strongly built — who came to be called Chalukya, with his descendants named Chalukyas.\n*   The **Kautham inscription** of Kalyani Chalukya ruler Vikramaditya V gives yet another version: Chalukyas belonged to Chandravamsa kings of Ayodhya. After 59 kings ruled Ayodhya, they went into exile in South India; after 16 more kings ruled there, that kingdom too declined. Some generations later, Jayasimhavallabha defeated the Rashtrakutas and fully re-established Chalukya authority.\n\n**2\\. The \"Chalika\" tribal-name theory**\n\n*   The word \"Chalika\" first appears in a third-century C.E. inscription of the Ikshvakus at Nagarjunakonda, describing Khandachelika Remmanaka, a feudatory of the Ikshvakus, as king of Hiranya rashtra (Cuddapah district). If \"Chalika\" is an old form of \"Chalukya,\" this would read as \"Chalukya Remmanaka.\"\n*   Chalukya inscriptions show variant forms of the word — Saliki, Saluki, Chelka, Chelalya, Chalukya — suggesting it was originally the name of a Dravidian-language tribe that later became the dynastic name.\n*   Dr. Rice felt Chalukyas may have belonged to the **family of Seleucus in Central Asia**, based on the similarity of the words \"Chalukya\" and \"Seleucus\" — an opinion not considered trustworthy, as similarity of sound is its only basis.\n*   D.C. Sircar held that the Chalukyas belonged to a **Kannada family** who declared themselves Kshatriyas and had a relationship with the Chulikas of Northern India, though no firm sources support this.\n*   The **Ranastapudi inscription** states the dynasty''s founder stayed in the ashram of Vishnubhatta Somayaji at Mudivemu agraharam (still existing in Cuddapah district — the Hiranya Rashtra of those times). The **Tummeyanoor inscription** of Pulakesi II similarly calls parts of Kurnool and Mahaboobnagar districts \"Chalukya Vishaya,\" on the model of how tribal/community names (Mulaka, Asaka, Andhra, Mahishaka) came to name their areas of habitation. Historians believe the native place itself became known as Chalukya Vishaya.\n*   In Maharashtra, a \"Chaliki\" caste exists near Bidar, where a village called Chalkapuri and a river called \"Chalki\" are also found.\n*   Chinese traveller Hiuen Tsang referred to Pulakesi II as \"**Maharastradhipati**.\" Based on this and other clues, Durga Prasad Dikshit proposed that the Bidar–Osmanabad region was the Chalukyas'' native land.\n*   Like the Kadambas, the Chalukyas referred to themselves as belonging to _manvya gothrikas_ and as _haratiputras_. The Chalukya royal names Pulakesi and Bittarasa are also held to belong to Karnataka usage — \"Pulakesi\" derives from _Polikesi_, meaning \"hair at the centre of the head\" (a hairstyle seen on ancient South Indian warrior sculptures, called _pulakesi_/_ashtavira_). The Kannada word _arasa_ (meaning king) appears in the names of Vengi and Vemulavada Chalukya kings and in some inscriptions — leading B.C. Sircar and other scholars to conclude the Chalukyas were of Karnataka origin.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p4", "partName": "Badami (Western) Chalukyas (543 – 752 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "### Background and Founding\n\nThe ancestors of the Chalukyas were feudatories of the Ikshvakus (who ruled Andhradesa from Vijayapuri) and ruled Hiranya rashtra in Cuddapah district — their native land. In the first half of the fourth century C.E., the Pallavas overthrew the Ikshvakus and occupied Andhradesa, and the Chalukyas were reduced to Pallava subordination. Resenting this, they tried to become independent; Trilochana Pallava attacked and defeated them.\n\nMayurasarma, the Kadamba ruler, was hostile to the Pallavas, so he allied with the Chalukyas, and together they fought the Pallavas before migrating into Karnataka and establishing a kingdom at Badami; their royal emblem was the **varaha (boar)**.\n\nThe founder of the Chalukya dynasty was **Jayasimha (Jayasimha Vallabha)**, who defeated the Rashtrakutas of Manyakheta. The Undikavatika copper plate of the Rashtrakuta ruler Abhimanyudu names a commander-in-chief called Jayasimha; it is opined this was the same Jayasimha Vallabha whom the Aihole inscription of Pulakesi II names as founder of the Chalukya kingdom. After Jayasimha, his son **Ranaraga** ascended the Chalukyan throne.\n\n### Rulers of Badami Chalukyas\n\n| Ruler | Reign |\n|---|---|\n| Pulakesi I | 535 – 566 A.D. |\n| Keertivarma | 566 – 597 A.D. |\n| Mangalesa | 598 – 609 A.D. |\n| Pulakesi II | 609 – 642 A.D. |\n| Vikramaditya | 642 – 680 A.D. |\n| Vinayaditya | 680 – 696 A.D. |\n| Vijayaditya | 696 – 733 A.D. |\n| Vikramaditya II | 733 – 744 A.D. |\n| Keertivarma II | 745 – 752 A.D. |\n\n### Pulakesi I (535 – 566 A.D.)\n\nSon of Ranaraga and founder of the independent Chalukyan dynasty. He raised Chalukyan authority from feudatory status to that of an independent kingdom, defied the Kadambas, occupied Vatapi, and built a strong fort there in 543 A.D., making it his capital. His victories are recorded in an inscription found at Badami (543 A.D.). To proclaim his triumphs, he performed an _asvamedha_ sacrifice. An inscription found at Yeleswaram suggests his kingdom extended up to Nalgonda district. He was the first Chalukyan ruler to assume the title \"maharaja.\"\n\n### Keertivarma (566 – 597 A.D.)\n\nSon of Pulakesi I, as capable and courageous as his father. His wife belonged to the Sendraka royal family. He defeated the Mauryas ruling the Konkan region and the Kadambas of Banavasi, extending his kingdom up to Konkan. He built several palaces and temples at Vatapi (Badami), earning praise from historians as the first ruler to beautify Vatapi as a city.\n\n### Mangalesa (598 – 609 A.D.)\n\nAs Keertivarma''s eldest son Pulakesi II was a minor at his father''s death, Keertivarma''s brother Mangalesa came to the throne. He defeated the Kadambas and conquered Revati island (Goa), expanding the kingdom, and held the title \"paramabhagavata.\"\n\nMangalesa tried to ensure the throne would pass to his own heirs after him, and this triggered a war of succession between Pulakesi II and Mangalesa, recorded in the **Hyderabad inscription** (612 A.D.), in which Mangalesa was killed and Pulakesi II ascended the throne in Saka era 581 / C.E. 609.\n\n### Pulakesi II (609 – 642 A.D.)\n\nThe greatest ruler among the Western Chalukya kings, comparable to North Indian emperors such as Chandragupta Maurya and Chandragupta I of the Gupta dynasty. With his rise to power, the \"Chalukyan age\" is said to have properly begun in South Indian history.\n\n**Conquests and expansion**\n\n*   Conducted several military expeditions and converted the Chalukyan kingdom — until then modest in extent — into a great empire spanning Maharashtra, Malwa, Kalinga, Andhra and Karnataka.\n*   His minister for war, Ravikeerthi, described his campaigns in the **Aihole (Ayyavolu) prasasti**, composed in Sanskrit. According to it, Pulakesi II first defeated the Kadamba, Ganga, Alupa, Maurya, Lata and Ghurjara kings t' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'o secure supremacy over South India.\n\n**Defeat of Harsha**\n\n*   On the banks of the river Narmada, Pulakesi II fought and defeated **Harshavardhana**, the \"Lord of Northern India,\" becoming the first Chalukyan ruler to check a North Indian emperor.\n*   The **Ayyavolu inscription** states Pulakesi II arranged his army most effectively and personally defeated Harshavardhana in this famous battle; afterward, the Narmada river became the recognized boundary between the two kingdoms.\n*   After this war, Pulakesi II assumed the title \"**Parameswara**.\"\n\n**Eastern campaigns and Vengi**\n\n*   After settling conquered northern areas under the control of his brothers, Pulakesi II began an eastward military expedition, conquering Kosala and Kalinga and raiding Vengi.\n*   Disordered political conditions then prevailing in Andhra land aided his campaign greatly; most areas of Telangana and Rayalaseema had already come under his control, though the lower Krishna river areas were under Pallava occupation and Vengi kingdom was under the Durjayas.\n*   Entering via Kalinga, Pulakesi first fought and defeated the Durjayas in the Pishtapuram and Kunala battles and occupied Vengi. His donations in Andhra are known from his Kopparam and Maratoor inscriptions.\n\n**Conflicts with the Pallavas**\n\n*   Pulakesi II advanced to Kanchipuram, the Pallava capital, and in a fierce battle at Pullalur defeated Pallava **Mahendravarma** (C.E. 600–630).\n*   When Mahendravarma I died, Pulakesi II again invaded, aiming to bring the Pallava kingdom fully under his control; the Banas of Rayalaseema, in this connection, came under Chalukyan control.\n*   Battles at Periyala, Manimangalam and Suramara followed between Pallava and Chalukya forces, with many Pallava areas coming under Pulakesi II.\n\n**Death**\n\n*   During the reign of Pallava Narasimhavarma I (630–668 A.D.), the battles took an extreme turn, and Pulakesi II died at Pallava hands; Narasimhavarma advanced to Badami, diminished its importance, and assumed the title \"**Vatapikonda**,\" in a battle fought in C.E. 642 at Manimangalam.\n    \n*   Though his royal authority thus ended, Pulakesi II is still regarded as the most warlike ruler in South Indian history.\n    \n\n**Other notable facts**\n\n*   The Persian emperor sent **Khusru as an ambassador** to Pulakesi II''s court; a painting in the Ajanta caves depicts this Persian embassy.\n*   Chinese traveller Hiuen-tsang visited his court in 641 A.D. and recorded that the Chalukyan kingdom was extensive, wealthy and prosperous, and that Pulakesi was a kshatriya commanding a great army.\n*   With Pulakesi II''s death and Narasimhavarma''s aggression, Badami''s prestige declined; Pallava armies looted the kingdom and created chaos. His sons — **Adityavarma, Chandraditya, Vikramaditya and Jayasimha** — failed to remain united in defending the kingdom from enemy attacks.\n\n### Vikramaditya (642 – 680 A.D.)\n\nThird son of Pulakesi II. He steadied and saved the kingdom from Pallava attacks, and conquered the Pandya, Chola and Kalabhra rulers who had earlier assisted the Pallavas. During his reign, Chalukya–Pallava conflict continued, and Chalukyan authority was extended up to \"three seas.\" He appointed his brother Jaysimhavarma, who had cooperated in his campaigns, as viceroy of the Lata (South Gujarat) kingdom.\n\n### Vinayaditya (680 – 696 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vikramaditya; ruled the inherited kingdom effectively and peacefully, developing it further. His inscriptions record an invasion of Northern India; he fought many battles and received gifts from the Kalabhra, Kerala, Haihaya, Malwa, Chola and Pandya kings. He was finally killed in the \"**Vilande**\" battle at the hands of the armies of Prameswaravarman, the Ganga king.\n\n### Vijayaditya (696 – 733 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vinayaditya; invaded with a large army and won against several North Indian royal dynasties. He adopted the Ganga-Yamuna torana and the **Pala flag** as his official emblem. As Vinayaditya had died at Badami, Vijayaditya took up kingly duties in C.E. 696. He built temples at Pattadakal (Kesuval).\n\n### Vikramaditya II (733 – 744 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vijayaditya. During his reign, he confronted the rising power of the Arabs and their attacks in the Sindh region. His contemporary, Pallava **Nandivarma II** (C.E. 695–722), assumed the title \"**Pallavamalla**\"; the Badami Chalukyas defeated the Pallavas in several battles fought between the two. He defeated the Pandya, Chola, Kerala and Kalabhra kingdoms, and as a mark of victory erected a pillar on the coast of the Southern Ocean. His wives, Lokamahadevi (of the Kalachuri dynasty) and Rajaneetrailokyadevi, had the temples \"Lokeswaralayam\" and \"Tralokeswaralayam\" built at Pattadakal in their names.\n\n### Keertivarma II (745 – 752 A.D.) — End of the Badami Chalukyas\n\nSon of Vikramaditya II; the last ruler of the Western Chalukya (Badami/Vatapi) dynasty. During his rule, Chalukyan authority began to decline as he was made to confront many critical conditions:\n\n*   Rashtrakuta **Dantidurga** not only occupied the Ellora region but also turned the Chalukya feudatory kings of Kosala, Kalinga and the Telugu Cholas toward himself.\n*   As Dantidurga''s power rose and Chalukya feudatories defected to him, in C.E. 752 he overthrew the Badami Chalukya dynasty and established the Rashtrakuta kingdom in Karnataka.\n\nInscriptions explaining the decline of Badami Chalukya authority — the \"**Aviyar rock slab**\" and \"**the Meraj plates**\" — are found in the surrounding areas of Maharashtra.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p5", "partName": "Eastern Chalukyas (Vengi Chalukyas) (624 – 1075 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Eastern Chalukyas stand among the dynasties that rendered the most valuable service to Telugu culture and literature. Because the Vengi region — lying between the Krishna and Godavari rivers — became their capital area, they are also called Vengi Chalukyas. Among the kingdoms that sprang from the mother line of the Badami Chalukyas, Eastern Chalukyas became the leading one, ruling Andhra land for four and a half centuries (C.E. 624 to 1075) with a highly praiseworthy history. Though considered \"non-Andhras\" by origin, by settling in Vengi and administering in the local language and with local feeling, they safeguarded Telugu culture — even while dynasties such as the Cholas and Chalukyas, fighting each other for supremacy, turned Andhra land into a battlefield, the Vengi Chalukyas tried to uphold the independence, honour and prestige of Andhra land.\n\n### Establishment and Three Phases of Rule\n\nThere were different opinions regarding the exact date of establishment, but as per Vengi Chalukya inscriptions, their rule at Vengi commenced in 624 A.D. Their rule (C.E. 624–1075) is divided into three phases:\n\n| Phase | Period | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| First | C.E. 624 – 753 | Peace and tranquility |\n| Second | C.E. 753 – 973 | Deadly enmity between the Vengi Chalukyas and the Rashtrakutas |\n| Third | C.E. 973 – 1075 | Decline of Rashtrakuta power; emergence of the Kalyani Chalukyas |\n\nThough aggression on Vengi occurred at different times, effective Chalukya authority over Vengi began only from C.E. 624 — when the Badami Chalukya king **Pulakesi II**, after conquering the Vengi region, appointed his brother **Kubja Vishnuvardhana** as viceroy there. Kubja Vishnuvardhana came to be regarded as the founder of the Vengi Chalukya kingdom.\n\n### Kubja Vishnuvardhana (624 – 641 A.D.)\n\nEastern Chalukyan inscriptions record that he ruled Vengi for 18 years. After becoming viceroy, the Vengi kingdom became independent and expanded. He bore titles such as \"**Vishamasiddhi**,\" \"**Kamadheva**\" and \"**Makaradhwaja**\" — the \"Vishamasiddhi\" title reflecting his fame for subjugating impregnable enemy fortresses.\n\n*   Up to C.E. 631, he ruled Vengi as representative of his brother, Pulakesi II. The **Kopparam inscription of C.E. 631** records that Pulakesi II permitted him to rule independently; his own inscriptions (found at Cheepurupalli and Timmapuram) confirm his kingdom extended from Visakhapatnam in the north to Kammanadu in the south, and that he had declared himself maharaja.\n*   From his time, feudatory kingdoms began to form within his territory; he honoured servants who helped him in war by granting them small kingdoms. Among the feudatories, he defeated **Indravarma III**, the Ganga dynasty ruler of Kalinga, and made him his feudatory.\n*   The **Timmapuram inscription** praises him as a devotee of Vishnu and as \"Paramabhagavata.\" Even so, he also came to be known as \"Kartikeya Kaki\" (devotee of goddess Durga). His wife, **Ayyanamahadevi**, favoured Jainism, and built a Jain monastery at Bezawada (Vijayawada) for Jain monks, donating a village for it.\n*   Chinese traveller **Hiuen-tsang** (C.E. 600–644) visited and recorded conditions of the time.\n*   Pulakesi II made two attacks on the Pallava kingdom during C.E. 631–641; in the last expedition, Pallava Narasimhavarma attacked Vatapi, and historians opine that Kubja Vishnuvardhana took part in support of his brother and died along with him.\n*   Kubja Vishnuvardhana was a skilful troop leader, an unrivalled warrior, and capable of subjugating any impregnable fortress — plain, forest or hillock. After him, his eldest son, **Jayasimha I**, ascended the throne in C.E. 642.\n\n### Jayasimha I (642 – 673 A.D.)\n\nKnown as Jayasimhavallabha I — the first Eastern Chalukyan king to bear the name \"Jayasimha.\" Chalukya inscriptions record he reigned 33 years. His titles were \"Sakala Lokasraya\" and \"Sarvasiddhi.\" Though his rule was largely peaceful, the Pulamboor inscription records some battles; he stabilised his supremacy over the Boya kings in the Nellore region.\n\n**Significance for Telugu:** During his reign, an inscription in the Telugu language was issued at Vipparla village (Narasaraopet mandal, Guntur district) — the **first Telugu inscription of the Eastern Chalukyas**, showing that Telugu was already gaining prominence from the time of Jayasimhavallabha I. He ruled with great splendour and died in C.E. 673.\n\n### Indrabhattaraka (673 A.D.)\n\nAs Jayasimhavallabha I had no children, his brother Indrabhattaraka came to the throne and ruled for only seven days. In this brief week, he issued the **Kondanagoor inscription**. His titles were \"tyagadhanudu\" and \"makaradhwaja.\"\n\n### Vishnuvardhana II (673 – 682 A.D.)\n\nSon of Indrabhattaraka. People praised him as \"**dharmasastravetta.**\" His titles included \"vishamasiddhi,\" \"makaradhwaja,\" \"sarvalokesaraya\" and \"pralayaditya\" — the \"vishamasiddhi\" title showing he had assumed the titles of Kubja Vishnuvardhana.\n\n### Mangi Yuvaraju (682 – 706 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vishnuvardhana II; ruled for 25 years with the title \"**vijayasiddhi.**\" His inscriptions show he expanded the kingdom from Visakhapatnam to Ongole. He had many wives, and from them three sons and a daughter (named Prithvipori).\n\n### Jayasimha II (706 – 718 A.D.)\n\nEldest son of Mangi Yuvaraju. From his time onward, **wars of succession began**, weakening the Chalukyan kingdom. As he had no children, after him his brothers **Kokkili Maharaju** (C.E. 718–19) and then **Vishnuvardhana III** ascended the throne.\n\n### Vishnuvardhana III (719 – 755 A.D.)\n\nBrother of Kokkili Maharaju. A fight took place between the two brothers for authority over the kingdom; after ruling six months, when Vishnuvardhana III rebelled, Kokkili fled to the central Kalinga region of Elemanchili. During these disturbances, Boya Kottalu in the Nellore region was taken back under Pallava control. He bore the title \"tribhuvanamkusa\"; his wife was named Vijayamahadevi. He issued many copper-plate inscriptions.\n\n### Vijayaditya I (755 – 772 A.D.)\n\nHis reign is important as marking the entry of the Eastern Chalukyas into their **second phase**. Around C.E. 755, the Rashtrakutas under **Dantidurga** ended the Badami Chalukya dynasty and established a new empire with Manyakheta as its capital (their first place of habitation was Latur village in Hyderabad province).\n\nWith the rise of the Rashtrakutas, the Chalukya–Rashtrakuta wars began, continuing for around 200 years and leaving history \"tainted with blood\"; though the Chalukyas made many efforts to confront the Rashtrakutas — even taking advantage of internal conflicts in the enemy camp — they could not escape defeat, and Rashtrakutas under Arikesari I, who ruled Vemulavada in Telangana, attacked Vengi-trikalinga, so that Vengi was at last made to accept the suzerainty of Manyakheta. Vijayaditya I bore the title \"tribhuvanamkusa.\"\n\n### Vishnuvardhana IV (772 – 808 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vijayaditya I; ruled Vengi, starting his rule as a feudatory of Rashtrakuta king **Krishna I** (as his father had been defeated). When Krishna I died in C.E. 722 \\[as per source\\], a war of succession broke out among his sons, and **Dhruva** ascended the throne in C.E. 780.\n\n*   In the succession war, Dhruva''s brother **Govinda** was helped by Vishnuvardhana IV. Dhruva sent **Arikesari I** (Vemulavada Chalukya, ruling Vemulavada in Telangana) against Vishnuvardhana IV, who had helped Govinda. Arikesari defeated Vishnuvardhana. The **Parbhani inscription (C.E. 966)** informs that Arikesari conquered Vengi and Kalinga.\n*   Vishnuvardhana accepted Rashtrakuta sovereignty and gave his daughter **Seelamahadevi** in marriage to Dhruva. But even such political marriage alliances could not end the deep-seated enmity between the two dynasties.\n\n### Vijayaditya II (808 – 847 A.D.)\n\nAlso known as Narendra Mrigaraju. He fought endless battles with Rashtrakuta feudatories — the Western Ganga and Vemulavada Chalukyas — staying in the battlefield day and night, sword in hand.\n\n*   When the Rashtrakutas interfered in the war of succession between him and Bhimasaluki, the prestige of the Vengi kingdom suffered; despite this, with the help of Nruparudra of the Haihaya dynasty, he fought 108 battles over more than twelve years against the supporters of Bhimasaluki, and at last occupied Vengi.\n    \n*   For these victories he earned the **titles** \"Narendreswara,\" \"Narendramrugaraju,\" \"Chalukyaraya\" and \"Vikramadavali.\"\n    \n*   Rashtrakuta king **Amoghavarsha**, who ascended the throne while still young, could not resist Vijayaditya II''s strength. **Karkaraju**, a Gujarat feudatory safeguarding the Rashtrakuta kingdom, accepted Vijayaditya''s supremacy and gave his daughter Seelamahadevi in marriage to Kalivishnuvardhana, Vijayaditya''s son. The Ghurjara-Prathiharas were defeated in Central India, and his remaining rule went on peacefully.\n    \n*   He is credited with building Siva temples at the 108 places where he fought his battles — installing a Sivalinga and constructing a Siva temple at each battlefield. It is said ' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'Bezawada (Vijayawada) got its name because of him.\n\n### Kalivishnuvardhana (847 – 848 A.D.)\n\nSon of Vijayaditya II (also called Vishnuvardhana V). Had four sons: Vijayaditya III, Vikramaditya, Nripakamudu and Yuddhamalla.\n\n### Gunaga Vijayaditya (Vijayaditya III) (849 – 892 A.D.)\n\nThe greatest among the Eastern Chalukyas — also called Gunaka Vijayaditya for his many good qualities. He was the ruler who could make the victory-flag of the Chalukyas march \"from the Ganges in the North to the Cauvery in the South.\" Around four decades were spent in wars and crisis.\n\n**Consolidation and administration**\n\n*   To suppress feudatories and establish peace, he appointed brahmin commanders-in-chief of highest loyalty, such as **Pandurangadu**, **Vinayadisarma** and **Rajaditya**.\n*   Pandurangadu conquered Boya Kottas, held Nellore and established peace there; a strong fort was built with Kandukoor as the central point, equivalent in importance to Bezawada.\n*   Though his mother **Seelamahadevi** was a Rashtrakuta princess, stable peace did not follow between the two dynasties for various reasons. As long as Amoghavarsha lived, the Chalukyas (under Gunaga) remained subordinate to Rashtrakuta authority.\n\n**Military campaigns**\n\n*   Conquered Gangavadi and Nolambavadi in C.E. 870, defeating Ranavikrama, the Gangavadi ruler, at Sivaganga. Rashtrakutas and their allies who supported them fell into trouble at the hands of Gunaga''s commander-in-chief. Forts like Achalapuram, Kiranapuram and Chakrakutam got destroyed. The Dahala kingdom (between the Ganga and Narmada rivers), South Kosala and Kalinga kingdoms were subjugated by Chalukyan armies.\n*   Achieving great victory and revenge, Rashtrakuta king Krishna had no alternative but to accept Gunaga Vijayaditya''s authority. Gunaga took charge of the Pali dhvaja and the Krishna–Yamuna torana, which remained symbols of Eastern Chalukya supreme authority.\n*   During his reign, Eastern Chalukyan authority extended from Mahanandi to Pulicat lake, remaining unrivalled in South Indian politics.\n*   After the death of Amoghavarsha (C.E. 880), his son **Krishna II** assumed authority but was defeated by Gunaga Vijayaditya; Krishna II had no alternative but to conclude peace with Gunaga and accept his sovereign authority (details recorded in the Dharmavaram, Attili and Paderu inscriptions).\n*   The **Sataluru inscription** of Gunaga Vijayaditya records victories against the Pallavas and Pandyas. Defeating the Pallavas and Pandyas, Vijayaditya may have saved a Chola king (possibly Aditya Chola) who had sought Pandurangadu''s shelter. Some inscriptions suggest Baddega, the Vemulavada king, was also defeated by him.\n*   Making the Rashtrakutas — themselves once conquerors of the entire South India — his feudatories, Gunaga Vijayaditya became the absolute and supreme sovereign of the entire South India. He assumed **titles** such as \"tripuramartamaheswara,\" \"parachakrarama\" and \"ranarangasudraka.\"\n\n**Decline after his death:** After him, quarrels started in the Vengi Chalukya dynasty over the throne. Taking advantage of these internal disturbances, enemies captured many occupied areas: the Ganga king **Vajrahasta** annexed the Trikalinga kingdom into his own. This brought down the prestige of the Vengi kingdom; the fact that divisions arose within the core strength of the Vengi Chalukyas showed how terrible conditions in the kingdom had become.\n\n### Chalukya Bhima I (892 – 921 A.D.)\n\nHis accession was opposed by his uncle **Yuddhamalla I** (his father Gunaga Vijayaditya''s brother), who sought help from Rashtrakuta Krishna. His rule did not go on peacefully.\n\n*   Vemulavada king **Baddega**, insulted during Gunaga Vijayaditya''s time, came with Rashtrakuta armies and captured Chalukya Bhima in the war fought at Kolleru — possibly when Bhima took shelter in the Kolleru water fort.\n*   The **Koravi copper plate inscription (C.E. 935)** informs that, to defeat the Rashtrakuta army, Bhima made **Mahakala**, son of his stepmother, associate with him as Dandanadha (commander-in-chief). Bhima defeated the Rashtrakutas and drove them away.\n*   In a fierce battle, **Irimartiganda**, the son of Bhima, killed a Rashtrakuta in the Nidadavolu and Peravanguru battles and sacrificed himself on the battlefield; as a memorial to his heroic son, Bhima made Vedatalur village an agrahara and donated it.\n*   Chalukya Bhima installed the Bheemeswaraswamy (temple) at Draksharamam.\n*   Around C.E. 900, during his time, the Kanchipura Pallava empire was conquered by the Cholas.\n*   His inscriptions record that during his 30-year rule he waged 360 battles.\n\n### Vijayaditya IV / Kollabhiganda (921 A.D.)\n\nSon of Chalukya Bhima I; ruled for only six months in C.E. 921. When **Vajrahasta III** of the Kalinga Ganga dynasty rebelled, the **Viraja battle** was fought against him, in which Vijayaditya IV was defeated and, it appears, died.\n\n### Ammaraju I (921 – 927 A.D.)\n\nAscended after Vijayaditya IV. It is said Rajamahendrapuram was constructed during his reign and became the Chalukyan capital — as Vengi, being in a flat plain, was easily subjected to enemy attacks, Ammaraju established his capital on the northern coast of the Godavari, bearing the title \"rajamahendra.\"\n\n**Wars of succession:** After Ammaraju''s death, succession wars started among the brothers of Gunaga Vijayaditya — Vikramaditya and Yuddhamalla. Up to Ammaraju I, only the descendants of Vikramaditya had ascended the Vengi throne, but Yuddhamalla''s line refused to tolerate this and declared war. **Talaraju** (C.E. 927), son of Yuddhamalla I, and then his son **Yuddhamalla II** (C.E. 928–934), occupied the kingdom. Yuddhamalla II is said to have shifted his capital to Bezawada out of insecurity, built a Kartikeya temple there, and issued the famous **Bezawada stone inscription (C.E. 930)**.\n\n### Chalukya Bhima II (934 – 945 A.D.)\n\nHalf-brother of Ammaraju I; drove away Yuddhamalla II and occupied the throne. He killed his competitors Tatabikki, Rajamarthanda, Lovabikki, Ayyapa and others who had occupied the throne. He could to some extent limit Rashtrakuta influence in Vengi politics. His wife Ankidevi, daughter of the Kalinga king, may have provided him Rashtrakuta-side help. During his time, the rebellion of the feudatories (Vemulavada Chalukyas) within the Rashtrakuta kingdom against Govinda IV worked to his advantage. He had two sons — Ammaraju II and Danarnava.\n\n### Ammaraju II (945 – 970 A.D.)\n\nRuled Vengi for 25 years, though dethroned twice. His mother was Lokamahadevi.\n\n*   After his coronation, the grandsons of Yuddhamalla I — **Badaparaju** (C.E. 945) and **Talapa II** — drove him away and occupied the kingdom for some time.\n*   Rashtrakuta **Krishna III** then sent an army against Ammaraju; Ammaraju''s kingdom was in turn occupied by Krishna III. After Krishna III turned back, Ammaraju returned to Vengi, occupied the kingdom, and reigned up to C.E. 970.\n*   He gave donation at Bezawada to a Saiva temple called \"samastabhuvanasraya,\" built by Narendramrigaraju. During his time, the Kalamukha sect developed.\n*   At the end, in C.E. 970, his brother **Danarnava** raided him; first defeated, Danarnava sought the help of the Mudigonda Chalukyas and, along with Rashtrakuta Krishna III, killed Ammaraju II and ascended the Vengi throne.\n\n### Danarnava (970 – 973 A.D.)\n\nHis mother, Urjapaya, was a princess from Kalinga. Though Krishna III had helped him gain the throne, Danarnava was able to conquer Southern Andhra, which had been lost by the Vengi Chalukyas. Taking advantage of the decline of Rashtrakuta power, he invaded Renadu in C.E. 972, took Velanadu, Konidesa and Pottapinadu under his control, and gifted away Pottapinadu to the Mudigonda Chalukyas who had helped him — thereby gaining rule over 300 villages.\n\nWhile Danarnava was ruling ably, **Jatachoda Bhima**, a relative of Ammaraju II, attacked him, killed him, and declared himself king.\n\n### Jatachoda Bhima (973 – 999 A.D.)\n\nWith his reign, Vengi political history entered its **third stage**. He dashed into Vengi politics; the inscription at the Kailasanatha temple gives information about him. He ruled with **Peddakallu** (Kurnool district) as his capital. Jatachoda was his father, and Vallabhiganda (daughter of Vijayaditya, granddaughter of Chalukya Bhima I, of the Chola dynasty) his mother.\n\n*   The killing of Ammaraju II and Danarnava''s seizure of the Vengi kingdom angered Jatachoda Bhima, who attacked Vengi, killed Danarnava, and occupied it. He is not mentioned in Vengi Chalukyan inscriptions.\n*   Danarnava''s sons, **Saktivarma and Vimaladitya**, fled to Kalinga and, with the help of Ganga Kamarnava, invaded Vengi; to confront them Jatachoda Bhima fought unending battles on all sides and defeated the Kalinga forces.\n*   As his authority strengthened in Vengi and Kalinga, Saktivarma and Vimaladitya, not knowing what to do, ran away to the Chola kingdom for shelter. Chola king **Rajaraja Chola** (C.E. 985–1013) then ruled, and clashes had begun between the Cholas and the Western Chalukyas of Karnataka for South Indian supremacy.\n*   Realizing that resisting Western Chalukya aggression required Chola predominance in Vengi, Rajaraja strengthened political ties by giving his daughter Kundavva in marriage to Vimaladitya; with Chola help, Saktivarma re-entered Vengi and warred with Jatachoda Bhima, and these wars are recorded in the Prabhuparru and Penneru inscriptions.\n*   Jatachoda Bhima drove away Saktivarma and the Chola army up to Kanchipuram, but died near Kanchipuram itself in the battle fought there, in C.E. 999. Vengi and Kalinga then came under Saktivarma''s control.\n\n### Saktivarma (1000 – 1011 A.D.)\n\nCame to the throne in C.E. 1000 after defeating Jatachoda Bhima; Vengi and Kalinga came under his control. His brother **Vimaladitya** (C.E. 1011–1018) succeeded him.\n\n*   Both brothers were anxious to bring Vengi fully under their own control, and Chalukyan prestige began to decline as the balance of power in South Indian politics was disrupted by growing Chola dominance in Vengi (unacceptable to the Western Chalukyas).\n*   Clashes began between Chola Rajaraja (C.E. 985–1013) and the Western Chalukya ruler; Vengi became the centre for these clashes. Law and order declined completely, exposing the weakness of the Vengi Chalukyas and causing much political damage to Vengi.\n*   Saktivarma bore the title \"**Chalukya Chandrudu**\"; coins with this title have been found on the Arakan coast, indicating traders of the day travelled to eastern islands for trade.\n\n### Vimaladitya (1011 – 1018 A.D.)\n\nBecame king after Saktivarma''s death; his coronation took place in May, 1011 A.D., with titles \"rajamarthanda\" and \"mummidibhima.\" Details of his eight-year rule are sparse. Some historians hold that Karnataka forces attacked Vengi during his time; unable to resist, he fled to Cholamandalam and returned with his uncle''s support to regain the kingdom.\n\nThough a Saiva in the beginning, he later accepted **Jainism**; Desiganacharya, \"trikalayogi\" and \"siddhantadeva\" were his gurus at the time of his initiation into Jainism. He built a cave temple called Ramakonda at Ramathirtham (Visakhapatnam mandalam).\n\n### Rajaraja Narendra (1019 – 1061 A.D.)\n\nVimaladitya had two wives: **Kundavva**, daughter of Chola emperor Rajaraja, and **Melima**, princess of the Telugu Choda dynasty. Kundavva gave birth to **Rajaraja Narendra**; Melima gave birth to **Vijayaditya**.\n\n**Succession dispute and Chola intervention**\n\n*   After Vimaladitya''s death, his son Rajaraja Narendra was to assume kingship, but as the kingdom did not come under his control, his coronation was held only after four years.\n*   His step-brother **Vijayaditya** disliked Rajaraja Narendra''s accession and allied with **Jayasimha II** (the Karnataka Chalukya emperor) and **Madhukamarnava** (the Kalinga Ganga king), occupying Vengi.\n*   Rajaraja Narendra, unable to oppose them, went to Cholamandalam as his ancestors had done and sought Chola help. By then his maternal grandfather Rajaraja Chola had died, and his uncle, **Rajendrachola**, was ruling the empire. Feeling pity for his nephew''s distress and troubled by Vengi''s fall into other hands, emperor **Rajendra Chola** invaded and defeated Vijayaditya and his allies, driving them from Vengi and celebrating Rajaraja Narendra''s coronation on **16th August, C.E. 1022**.\n*   Rajendra Chola''s daughter, Ammangadevi, was Rajaraja Narendra''s wife.\n\n**Ongoing conflict**\n\n*   Many matters relating to his reign are unclear, but when the Cholas helped him, the Western Chalukyas and Kalinga (who did not tolerate Chola presence in Vengi) helped Vijayaditya instead.\n*   With Western Chalukya king **Aahavamalla Someswara** on one side and **Rajadhiraja** (who became the Chola ruler) on the other, mutual clashes intensified. Someswara conquered Vengi and Kalinga; his feudatories, such as Sobhanarasu, assumed the title \"Vengipuravareswara.\"\n*   Rajadhiraja made great, sustained efforts over more than ten years — more than ten battles were fought to destroy Chalukyan domination at Vengi. In the **Dharanikota battle**, Western Chalukya commanders-in-chief Gangadhara and Gandapayya were killed; Chola armies occupied territory up to Kollipaka.\n*   Details of the Chola–Western Chalukya wars in this period are not fully clear. When Rajadhiraja died in the **Kopparam war (C.E. 1053)**, this meant the loss of Chola protection for Rajaraja Narendra — leaving no option except to accept the supremacy of Ahavamalla Someswara.\n\n**Cultural patronage:** Just as his contemporary Bhojamaharaja patronised Sanskrit poets, Rajaraja Narendra patronised Telugu poets and won fame; his contribution to Telugu language was more remarkable even than Bhoja''s contribution to Sanskrit. **Nannaya**, the poet titled \"sabdasasana,\" adorned his court. The first two-and-a-half parvas of the _Andhra Mahabharata_ were written by Nannaya, assisted by **Narayanabhattu**.\n\n### Rajendra (Kulottunga Chola) and the End of Vengi Chalukyas (to 1075 A.D.)\n\nAfter Rajaraja Narendra''s death, his son **Rajendra** was to ascend the throne, but the details of the events at this time are unclear. His uncle (father''s step-brother) **Vijayaditya** (C.E. 1061–1075) occupied the throne, preventing Rajendra''s accession.\n\n*   Rajendra''s uncle (mother''s brother), **Rajendra Chola II**, tried to drive Vijayaditya VII from Vengi and restore Rajendra to his father''s kingdom. After Rajendra Chola II, **Virarajendra** raided several times and, at last in C.E. 1067, conquered Vengi, Kalinga and Chakrakuta.\n*   It is not clear why Rajendra was not given his father''s kingdom; instead his uncle-rival Vijayaditya was preferred and kept in Chakrakuta mandal. Afterward, in a short period when Virarajendra died, Rajendra came fro' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'm Chakrakutam to Vengi, defeated Vijayaditya, and — in order to join Vengi to the Chola empire — came to war with Vijayaditya; but the latter received help from **Rajraja Devendra Varma**, the Eastern Ganga king, and **Yasahkarnadeva**, the Chedi king, and as a result, Rajendra''s army was defeated.\n*   Rajendra, in order to defeat Vijayaditya, made a plan of marrying his daughter, Rajasundari, to Devendravarma. As **Vijayaditya died in C.E. 1075**, the Vengi Chalukya dynasty came to an end. Rajendra, who had ascended the Chola throne with the title **Kulottunga Chola**, finally joined Vengi into the Chola empire.\n\n### Chola – Western Chalukya Relations in Vengi\n\nChola Rajaraja, keeping the interest of his empire in view and aiming to bring Vengi under his rule, gave shelter to Saktivarma and Vimaladitya. He gave Vimaladitya his daughter Kundavva in marriage, and made Saktivarma the king of Vengi — turning the Vengi kingdom into a Chola-protected kingdom.\n\nChola influence in Vengi became an eyesore to the Kalyani Chalukyas, who had their eye on sovereign authority over South India. With Vengi remaining the battleground between the Chola and Kalyani Chalukyas, many battles took place. Eastern Chalukya king Saktivarma turned back a Kalyani Chalukya attack with Chola help; his brother Vimaladitya married Chola princess Kundavva, and also Melima, daughter of Jatachoda Bhima.\n\nThe coronation of Rajaraja Narendra was delayed due to the conflicting efforts of the Kalinga king Madhukamarnava (desiring to free himself from Vengi Chalukya hegemony), the Kalyani Chalukya king Jayasimha (aiming to end Chola dominance), and — as a climax — Vijayaditya VII planning to occupy the Vengi throne with the help of both these rulers. Vijayaditya''s efforts to occupy Vengi with Chola help were foiled by Rajara Narendra.\n\nAs Jayasimha, the Kalyani Chalukya king, died, his son **Ahavamalla Someswara** ascended the throne. Later, when Rajendra Chola died, **Rajadhiraja** came to the Chola throne. During the time of these two rulers, Chalukya–Chola conflict reached a high pitch, and Vengi witnessed chaos. Ahavamalla foiled Chola efforts and established Kalyani Chalukyan dominance at Vengi.\n\nWith the death of Ahavamalla (C.E. 1068), internal struggle started among his sons. Afterward, Virarajendra died within two years, and in a few weeks his son Adhirajendra also died. Eastern Chalukyan king Rajendra occupied Gangaikonda Cholapuram and, with the name Kulottunga Chola, ascended the throne. After the death of **Vijayaditya VII (C.E. 1075)**, Vengi finally became joined to the Chola empire.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p6", "partName": "Vemulavada Chalukyas (750 – 973 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Vemulavada Chalukyas were feudatories of the Rashtrakutas, ruling the north-western Telangana region with **Vemulavada** (Karimnagar district) as their capital. As feudatories of the Rashtrakutas, they ruled from C.E. 750 to 975 — around 225 years. Their rule, which started with the kindness of the Rashtrakutas, also ended with the Rashtrakutas.\n\n**Sources:** Six stone inscriptions and two copper-plate inscriptions are the main sources for their history — the Kuruvagattu stone inscription (Mahaboobnagar), the Karimnagar inscription (C.E. 946), and the Vemulavada stone inscriptions issued by Arikesari II. The Chennur stone inscription (C.E. 941) of Arikesari II records that Yuddhamalla I was opposed by the Mudigonda Chalukya king, Vijayaditya; when the latter invaded him, he sought the help of Vemulavada king Arikesari II, who not only sheltered Vijayaditya but also made him administrator of the Chennur region. The Kurkyala inscriptions (C.E. 940) of Jinavallabha and the stone inscriptions in the Karimnagar museum reveal the genealogy of the Vemulavada Chalukyas; the Kollipara copper-plate inscription of Arikesari I and the Parbhani copper plate (C.E. 966) of Arikesari III help reconstruct their history. Literary works — **Vikramarjuna Vijayam** by Pampa, and **Yasastilaka Champukavyam** by the Jain scholar Somadevasuri (also useful for religious and social conditions and political details) — are additional sources. Hindu and Jain temples built at Repaka, Korutla and Bommalagutta serve as archaeological sources. Nelatoori Venkataramanaiah undertook integrated research on the Vemulavada kings and, after gathering the available information, wrote works in English and Telugu.\n\n### Early Rulers\n\nAs per their inscriptions, the founder of the dynasty was **Satyasraya Ranavikrama (C.E. 650–675)**. His sons **Prithvipathi (C.E. 675–700)** and **Maharaju (C.E. 700–725)**, and the latter''s son **Prithvivikrama (C.E. 725–750)**, all ruled, but details of their reigns, where they ruled, and the extent of the kingdom are not clearly known.\n\n### Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla (750 – 775 A.D.)\n\nFounder of the Vemulavada Chalukyan kingdom (as an established, better-documented line). The political history of the first four rulers of this branch, up to Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla, is not clear. He is described as a great conqueror. Historians opine he was the son of Dharasraya Jayasimhavarma; some argue he was the brother of Pulakesi only.\n\n*   He joined as commander-in-chief of **Dantidurga**, founder of the Rashtrakuta empire, and participated in his wars.\n*   He is said to have ruled **Sapadalaksha** (a kingdom of one lakh twenty-five thousand villages) — no one else would have ruled such a kingdom — had all his elephants bathed in an oil tank at Podana, conquered the impregnable Chitrakuta fort, and won Dantidurga''s appreciation.\n*   Impressed by his services, Dantidurga appointed him feudatory for the areas of Bodhan (Nizamabad) and Karimnagar; he ruled with **Bodhan** as his capital.\n*   Pampa''s _Vikramarjuna Vijayam_ mentions he had elephant forces in greater numbers, and that he conquered Turkish, Yavana, Barbara, Kashmira, Kamboja, Magadha, Kalinga, Ganga, Pallava, Pandya and Kerala areas. The Kollipara copper-plate inscriptions state that the rulers of these kingdoms worshipped at his feet.\n\n### Arikesari I (775 – 800 A.D.)\n\nAssumed authority after his father Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla''s death. He was a feudatory of Rashtrakuta **Dhruva** (C.E. 780–793).\n\n*   In the war of succession between Dhruva and his brother Govinda II, Arikesari I supported Dhruva; the Kollipara copper-plate inscription informs that when Eastern Chalukya ruler Vishnuvardhana IV helped Govinda, the wars fought as a reaction to this were led by Arikesari I, and in these wars, Vishnuvardhana gave his daughter Seelamahadevi in marriage to Rashtrakuta Dhruva.\n    \n*   As a mark of victory, the Kuruvagattu inscription records that Dhruva gifted Arikesari I the areas of Nagarkurnool in Mahaboobnagar district and Ramadugu in Nalgonda district. With this eastward extension, the capital shifted from Bodhan to **Vemulavada**.\n    \n*   Arikesari I made an educational donation of Belmoga village to Mugdhasivacharya, disciple of Kalamukha Saivacharya Sadyasivacharya. His kingdom extended to the regions of Nalgonda, Karimnagar and Mahaboobnagar.\n    \n*   The Kollipara inscription records he was a great scholar with remarkable prowess in Gajatantra, archery and Ayurveda, and held titles such as \"samastalokasraya,\" \"tribhuvanamalla,\" \"rajatrinetra\" and \"sahasrarayadi.\"\n    \n*   After Arikesari I, his sons in order — **Narasimha I (C.E. 800–825)** and the latter''s son **Yuddhamalla II (C.E. 825–850)** — ruled. During their fifty-year rule, no victories or developments of note occurred.\n\n### Baddega (850 – 895 A.D.)\n\nSon of Yuddhamalla II. Held the title \"**soladaganda**,\" meaning \"a warrior who knew no defeat\" — earned through victory in **forty-two battles**.\n\n*   The Parbhani inscription (C.E. 966) records he invaded Vengi, defeated Chalukya Bhima, and took him into custody \"just like catching a crocodile in a pond.\"\n*   Eastern Chalukyan inscriptions also state that Baddega defeated Gunaga Vijayaditya. After Gunaga''s death, in the revengeful war waged by Rashtrakuta **Krishna II**, Baddega played a leading role.\n*   But Kusumayudha, of the Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty, defeated Baddega, drove him away, got Chalukya Bhima released from captivity, and enabled him to ascend the throne — so despite his title \"soladaganda,\" Baddega could not avoid this defeat.\n    \n*   He built a temple in his own name at Vemulavada, known as the Baddegeswara temple — now famous as the Vemulavada **Bheemeswara temple**.\n*   After Baddega, his son **Yuddhamalla III** ruled from C.E. 895 to 915, though details of his reign are not available. After him, his son **Narasimha II (C.E. 915–930)** brought successes back to the dynasty.\n\n### Narasimha II (915 – 930 A.D.)\n\nCame to the throne in C.E. 915 as successor to Yuddhamalla III. A warrior of great strength who won many battles; through a military expedition up to the Ganges river, he secured a place of importance in the history of the age.\n\n*   He was the feudatory of Rashtrakuta **Indra III** (C.E. 915–922), participating in Indra III''s northern Indian military expedition. On Indra III''s orders, he invaded the northern region, conquered Lata and Saptamalava, and collected tribute. He later invaded Kanyakubjam, defeated **Mahipala** (C.E. 912–944), the Gurjara Prathihara ruler, and drove him away — an attack which, made with Indra III''s help, dealt a serious blow to the Prathihara kingdom from which it did not recover.\n*   The **Vemulavada inscription** records that as Mahipala fled, Narasimha II''s chasing horses bathed in the Ganga river; on the return march, as a mark of victory, he erected a victory pillar at Kalapriyam (opined by Altekar to be on the coast of the Yamuna river).\n*   Narasimha II and Indra III were friends, relatives and contemporaries — the wife of Narasimha II happened to be the sister of Indra III; her name was **Jakavva**, and she became the mother of **Arikesari II**. Appreciating Narasimha II''s victories, Indra III is thought to have married his sister to him.\n*   During his time, Jaina \"choumukhas\" (four-faced Jain images) were carved at Vemulavada. His military abilities and leadership were responsible for his extraordinary rise — \"an ordinary leader with limited authority could never dream about such elevation.\"\n\n### Arikesari II (930 – 955 A.D.)\n\nAmong the Vemulavada Chalukya rulers, Arikesari II earned the most name — admired as a scholar, warrior, good ruler, great organiser and patron of poets.\n\n**Political role**\n\n*   War of succession erupted in the Rashtrakuta kingdom during his time: when **Amoghavarsha II** (son of Indra III) ascended the throne, he was dethroned by his younger brother **Govinda IV**, who led a life without peace due to his cruelty and immoral behaviour, causing many enemies to spring up.\n*   His uncle Baddega and Krishna (son of Baddega) prepared plans for dethroning Govinda IV; parallel wars of succession also took place in Vengi after Chalukya Bhima I.\n*   **Vijayaditya** of Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty joined Arikesari II and fought the Rashtrakutas. Under these circumstances, Baddega was invited by Arikesari II to Vemulavada; he was declared Rashtrakuta emperor, defeated Govinda IV''s armies in many battles and drove him away, and Baddega (under the name **Amoghavarsha III**) was installed on the Rashtrakuta throne.\n\n**Patronage and achievements**\n\n*   Under his patronage, the **first great work in Kannada literature, Pampa''s \"Vikramarjuna Vijayam,\"** was written during his rule. **Aditya Griham** was built at Vemulavada.\n*   He married **Revakanirmadi**, daughter of Indra III and Lokambika, another Rashtrakuta princess.\n*   His titles: pambaramkusa, ammaanagandhavarana, udattanarayana, gunanidhi, gunarnava, saranagata, vajrapanjara, priyagala, tribhuvanamalla, samantachudamani.\n*   Of his four surviving inscriptions, the **Vemulavada inscription** (in Sanskrit) is the most important, giving the genealogy of the Vemulavada Chalukyas from Vinayaditya Yuddhamalla to Arikesari II. This same inscription states that **Peddanaryudu**, his war minister, built Adityalaya at Vemulavada, coming to the temple on Sankranti day to donate 100 nivaratnas of land.\n*   **Pampa** was his court poet, and in _Vikramarjuna Vijayam_ compared Arikesari II to Arjuna. Delighted with the work, Arikesari II gave Pampa Dharmapuri (Jagityal Taluka) as an agrahara.\n*   The Baddega inscription found at Chennur (Adilabad district, C.E. 941) states his kingdom extended from Bodhan to Chennur.\n*   He had two sons: **Vegaraju** (born to Revakanirmadi), the eldest, and **Baddega II** (born to Lokambika).\n\n### Vegaraju (955 – 960 A.D.)\n\nEldest son of Arikesari II, assumed royal authority — also a feudatory of Krishna III. He ruled the kingdom with **Gangadhara** town as his capital; this was located at Yelpadi (Chittoor district). Somadevasuri, in his chempukavya, states he completed writing his work in Saka era 881 (C.E. 959) during Vegaraju''s time (during the time of Arikesari II, per the source).\n\n### Baddega II / Bhadradeva (960 – 965 A.D.)\n\nAs Vegaraju had no children, his younger brother Baddega II (Bhadradeva) came to the throne. He issued an inscription at Vemulavada, and, for the sake of Somadevasuri, built a Jaina monastery of Gauda Sangha named **subhadamajenalaya**. During his time, Bommala gattu developed into a great Jaina centre.\n\n### Arikesari III (965 – 973 A.D.) — Last of the Vemulavada Chalukyas\n\nSon of Baddega II; the **last king of the Vemulavada Chalukya dynasty**. He issued the Parbhani copper-plate inscription (966 A.D.) and the Karimnagar copper-plate inscription (946 A.D.). Vemulavada was his capital.\n\n*   He granted Repaka village (in present-day Sircilla taluka) to \"subhadamajenalayam,\" which had been built by his father for Somadevasuri''s sake. The Repaka inscription (968 A.D.) confirms a Jaina temple was built there and granted land.\n*   His titles: pambaramkusa, vidyadhara, vikramarjuna, samantachudamani.\n\n**End of the dynasty:** In **973 A.D., he destroyed the Rashtrakuta dynasty and established the Kalyani Chalukya kingdom with Tailapa II as king.** Along with the Rashtrakutas, Vemulavada Chalukya rule also came to an end in 973 A.D.\n\nVemulavada Chalukyas ruled Karimnagar and Nizamabad districts as though it was a golden age; many Hindu and Jain temples were built in these areas, and the Rajarajeswara and Baddigeswara (Bhimeswara) temples became famous. Their role in Telangana history was remarkable.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p7", "partName": "Mudigonda Chalukyas (850 – 1200 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Mudigonda Chalukyas ruled over **Manchikondanadu in Koraviseema** (present Telangana), with **Mudigonda** (Khammam district) as their capital. The Khammam and Warangal areas came ' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'to be known as \"Koraviseema\" — the eastern part of which was called Manchikondanadu. Like the Vemulavada Chalukya kingdom, the Mudigonda kingdom lay on the borders of the Vengi and Malkhed empires and controlled all the routes entering Vengi. Mudigonda Chalukyas guarded the north-western borders of the Vengi Chalukyas and carried on their rule as trusted feudatories of the Vemulavada Chalukyas and their neighbours — the Eastern Chalukyas and Rashtrakutas.\n\n**Sources:** The Koravi copper-plate inscription (C.E. 935) and the Mogalcheruvu inscription cover the period up to the Eastern Chalukyan age; for later history, the Viriyalavari Gudoor inscription (C.E. 1124) and the Krivvaka inscription of Kusumayudha are the main sources. As these inscriptions have no dates and their information is inadequate, there was limited opportunity to know their full history.\n\n### Ranamardhudu — Founder\n\nFounder of the Mudigonda Chalukyan dynasty, possibly belonging to the Western Chalukyan family line. He acquired Koravi mandalam with the help of the Eastern Chalukyas and ruled from Mudigonda.\n\n### Kusumayudhudu / Kusumayudha I (C.E. 870 – 895)\n\nSon of Ranamardhudu (per the Koravi inscription). He was contemporary with Gunaga Vijayaditya and Chalukya Bhima, and played a leading role in the Vengi Chalukya–Rashtrakuta conflict of his time.\n\n*   After the death of Gunaga Vijayaditya, Rashtrakuta Krishna II, in a revengeful attitude, first occupied the Koravi region and then led an expedition against the Vengi Chalukyas; in response, Kusumayudha attacked the Rashtrakutas, freed Chalukya Bhima from Krishna II''s captivity, installed him on the throne, drove the Rashtrakutas from Koraviseema, and skilfully got the empire protected, and with the empire being handed back to Chalukya Bhima, Kusumayudha earned respect in several ways.\n    \n\n### Gonagaiah (895 – 910 A.D.)\n\nKusumayudha had two sons, Gonagaiah and Niravadya; out of regard for Gunaga Vijayaditya, one son was named \"Gonagaiah\" (the short form of Gunaga Vijayaditya). Gonagaiah was also called Karigonaga (\"black Gonaga\"). After Kusumayudha, Gonagaiah ascended the throne. Because of internal conflicts that arose in Vengi after Chalukya Bhima, Gonagaiah lost the affection of the Vengi Chalukyas.\n\nTaking advantage of these developments, his brother Niravadya (C.E. 910–935) removed Gonagaiah and himself ascended the Mudigonda Chalukyan throne, as recorded by the Koravi inscription; having lost the kingdom, Gonagaiah sought the help of Arikesari II (C.E. 930–955), and with his help, dethroned Niravadya and got the Mudigonda kingdom back. In the later period, only the descendants of Gonagaiah ruled over the Mudigonda kingdom.\n\n### The Emergence of the Kakatiyas and Mudigonda–Kakatiya Conflict\n\nIn this period, the **Kakatiya kingdom emerged**. Perhaps with the idea of keeping the Mudigonda Chalukyas in check, Rashtrakuta king Krishna II appointed his commander-in-chief **Yeriya** as administrator over **Kurravadi** (only part of the Koravi kingdom). Gundaya, the father of Yeriya, was a Rashtrakuta, referred to in the Machilipatnam inscription of Chalukya Bhima as Dandeva Gundana. Perhaps Yeriya made **Orugallu (present Warangal)** his capital — with this, the Kakatiya dynasty was installed.\n\n*   From this point, **Kakatiya–Mudigonda Chalukya conflict** began; the incidents of the first stage are not clearly known, but because of the rise of the Kakatiyas, the Chalukyas appear to have been forced temporarily to leave Koravi.\n*   Yeriya''s son was **Betiya**, and Betiya''s son was **Kakartiya Gundana**. It appears Betiya was driven away and the Mudigonda Chalukyas reoccupied Koraviseema. But, during their period, in the conflict that occurred for the Chalukyan throne (Vengi), **Danarnava** killed his younger brother Ammarajamahendra and ascended the Vengi throne. For helping in this, Danarnava made Kakartya Gundana ruler of **Natavadi**, and tried to send the Mudigonda Chalukyas to the Renati region.\n*   In the meantime, political conditions changed in both the Vengi and Rashtrakuta kingdoms: in Vengi, Danarnava was removed by **Jatachoda Bhima**; and in the Rashtrakuta kingdom, **Tailapa II** (Kalyani Chalukyas) removed the Rashtrakuta dynasty. In this situation, Kakartya Gundana invaded Koraviseema, defeated the Mudigonda Chalukyas, and reoccupied **Kurravadi kingdom**, which had been lost by his father Betiya.\n*   The Mudigonda Chalukyas, having lost their kingdom, sought Tailapa II''s help. As both were Chalukyan families, and the Kakatiyas had been their enemy commanders-in-chief, Tailapa II decided to remove the Kakatiyas and restore Koraviseema to the Mudigonda Chalukyas. Tailapa II entrusted this task to his commander, **Viriyala Yerrabhupati**, who defeated Kakartya Gundana, killed him, and — as stated in the Gudoor inscription (C.E. 1124) — installed **Bottu Betadu** as king in Koraviseema. According to the Krivvaka inscription, **Bottu Bethadu (C.E. 1125–50)**, installed by Yerrabhupati, is learnt to be of Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty. Either way, based on these two inscriptions, Bottu Bethadu of the Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty again became king of Koraviseema.\n\n**Later succession:** Due to Kakartya Gundana''s death, his son **Garuda Betaraju**, being a minor, lost the kingdom and became helpless. Seeing the minor child, **Kamasani**, wife of Yerrabhupati, took pity on him, sought Tailapa''s help, and could get the kingdom back to Betaraju. Tailapa gave the **Hanumakonda** area to Garuda Betaraju. As a result of this arrangement, the Kakatiyas in Warangal and the Mudigonda Chalukyas in Koraviseema got stabilised, and both became feudatories of Tailapa II.\n\n*   The **Palampeta inscription** informs that **Kakati Rudra** invaded the Mudigonda Chalukyas (feudatories of the Kalyani Chalukyas) and drove them away. In the later period, after the death of **Rudradeva**, the Mudigonda Chalukyas reoccupied Koraviseema.\n\n### Kusumayudha VI (1150 – 1175 A.D.) — Last of the Mudigonda Chalukyas\n\nLearnt from the Krivvaka inscription to be the **last ruler among the Mudigonda Chalukyas**. During the time of his younger brother, **Nagatiraju (C.E. 1175–1200)**, **Ganapatideva** (Kakatiya kingdom) invaded the Mudigonda Chalukyan kingdom, defeated him and drove him away.\n\nWith this, the Mudigonda Chalukyan kingdom became part of the Kakatiya empire; it is learnt that the last Mudigonda Chalukyas, having lost the kingdom, went to Vengi, stayed there for some time, and died — and with their death, the Mudigonda Chalukya dynasty, it is learnt, has ended.\n\n### Mudigonda Chalukya Genealogy\n\n| Ruler | Period |\n|---|---|\n| Ranamardha | 850 – 870 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha I | 870 – 895 A.D. |\n| Gonagaiah | 895 – 910 A.D. |\n| Niravadya | 910 – 935 A.D. |\n| Baddega | 935 – 941 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha II | 935 – 960 A.D. |\n| Vijayaditya II | 960 – 980 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha III | 980 – 1000 A.D. |\n| Gonaga | 1000 – 1025 A.D. |\n| Nijjayaraja Mallappa | 1025 – 1050 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha IV | 1050 – 1075 A.D. |\n| Betaraju | 1075 – 1100 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha V | 1100 – 1125 A.D. |\n| Bottu Beta | 1125 – 1150 A.D. |\n| Kusumayudha VI | 1150 – 1175 A.D. |\n| Nagatiraju | 1175 – 1200 A.D. |\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p8", "partName": "Kalyani Chalukyas (973 – 1157 A.D.)", "bodyMd": "Among the great dynasties that ruled South India and exercised influence over Andhradesa, the Kalyani Chalukyas deserve mention. Though the language of their inscriptions is Kannada and the area they mostly ruled was Karnataka, the works _Prabhanda Chintamani_ and _Rasamalaprabandham_, written by **Meruthunga Charyulu**, state that they belong to Telangana. Kalyani Chalukyas are also called **Western Chalukyas**, and belong to the Badami Chalukya dynastic line.\n\nThe Rashtrakuta empire, established after overthrowing the Badami Chalukya dynasty, continued for two and a half centuries and suffered decline at the hands of **Tailapa II**, who belonged to the Kalyani Chalukyan dynasty. The first capital of the Kalyani Chalukyas was **Manyakheta**; later they made **Kalyani** their capital and ruled from there for around 200 years. Their rule was established by Tailapa II.\n\n### Tailapa II (973 – 997 A.D.)\n\nBy 957 A.D. he was a feudatory of the Rashtrakutas, belonging to the dynasty of **Satyasraya Bhimarasu**, who ruled the Hanumakonda area as a Rashtrakuta feudatory. He was the son of Vikramaditya IV and Bontadevi. As Bontadevi was a Chedi princess, Tailapa may have secured Chedi help in overthrowing the Rashtrakutas. Some opine he belonged to the dynasty of Bhimarasu, uncle (father''s brother) of the last Badami Chalukya ruler Keertivarma II — from the title \"mahamandaleswara\" of Tailapa II, it appears he was a feudatory of the Rashtrakutas.\n\n*   By defeating **Karka II**, the last Rashtrakuta king, in **C.E. 973**, the sovereignty of the Kalyani Chalukyas was established. He had no titles until C.E. 957; by C.E. 965, he assumed the titles \"mahasamantadhipa,\" \"chalukyarama,\" \"satyasrayakula tilaka,\" \"ahavamalla,\" \"prithvi vallabha.\"\n*   Chola emperor Rajaraja was his contemporary. With Hanumakonda as capital and ruling over Western Telangana as the ancestral area — and as a feudatory under the Rashtrakutas, as known from inscriptions — the works _Prabhanda Chintamani_ and _Rasamala Prabhandam_ claim he was the head of Telangana. **Jakavva**, wife of Tailapa II, was the daughter of Karka II, the Rashtrakuta king.\n\n**Wars**\n\n*   In the north, **Munjaraja**, the Paramara king, was his enemy and invaded him sixteen times as a rival. Once Tailapa imprisoned Munjaraja, who escaped and fled; the last time, in C.E. 995, when Munjaraju crossed the Godavari, **Yadava Bhillama**, Tailapa''s feudatory, attacked and killed him in battle. Though Tailapa had imprisoned Munjaraja earlier, Mrunalavati, the widowed sister of Tailapa, loved Munjaraja and helped him escape; he was caught and killed, \"as ''prabhanda chintamani'' informs in a terrible manner.\"\n*   In the south, Tailapa II could not escape trouble from the Cholas — described in inscriptions as a \"devastating fire\" in respect of the Cholas, suggesting he fought several battles with them; enmity developed between Tailapa and the Cholas. When Rajaraja Chola occupied Mysore and began acting aggressively, **Tailapa in C.E. 992 defeated them and took away their elephant force as a gift.** He also defeated the Paramaras and Cholas, and conquered Lata, Ghurjara and Pallikota areas; the Banavasi area was joined to his kingdom.\n*   **Badaparaja**, of the Chalukya dynasty, was his commander-in-chief. **Ranna**, one of the \"kaviratnatrayam\" poets in Kannada literature, flourished in Tailapa''s court.\n\n### Satyasraya (997 – 1008 A.D.)\n\nEldest son of Tailapa II; bore the titles \"irivabendaga\" and \"akalamka charita.\" He ascended the Kalyani Chalukya throne in 997 A.D. As crown prince, remaining as his father''s right hand, he helped him in many battles — his title \"irivabendaga\" suggests that, in battle, he used to pierce himself toward the enemy army.\n\n*   During his reign, enmity with the Cholas became serious: Satyasraya did not tolerate the Cholas coming closer to Vengi, giving protection to **Saktivarma and Vimaladitya**, the two sons of Danarnava. Chola Rajaraja invaded Karnataka and conquered Gangavadi and Nolambavadi; as a result, his entire time was spent fighting continuous wars against the Cholas.\n*   His daughter Pampadevi was given in marriage to Pallava king Irivanolambadhi. To protect Vengi from Chalukyan armies, Rajaraja sent one army to Vengi to help Saktivarma, and another under his son Rajendra Chola on an expedition against Rattapadi.\n*   The **Mattur inscription (C.E. 1007)** informs that with a huge, fully-armed force stationed near Donapoor, people were butchered without discriminating women, children, the aged and brahmins, and the kingdom was looted. Chola inscriptions record that Banavasi and Kollipaka were conquered; the Chalukyan armies from Vengi were attacked again at Kollipaka by the Chola armies and were defeated — Satyasraya got subjected to some humiliation.\n*   **Vimalachandra**, a Jain scholar, was guru to Satyasraya.\n\n### Vikramaditya V (1008 – 1018 A.D.)\n\nAs Satyasraya had no sons, and his brother Dasavarna had died earlier, the younger brother''s son, Vikramaditya V, assumed royal authority in C.E. 1008 and ruled up to C.E. 1018, with the title \"tribhuvanamalla.\"\n\n*   Though **Nolambhadiraju**, the son-in-law of Satyasraya, accepted his supremacy, **Kundamarasu**, who was looking after Banavasi affairs, opposed him. As Kundamarasu described himself as \"death to devour the Cholas,\" it is assumed he also fought against the Cholas.\n*   Vikramaditya V was liberal and made donations; he had a sister named Akkadevi.\n\n### Jayasimha II (1018 – 1042 A.D.)\n\nAfter Vikramaditya V, his younger brother Ayyana became king but died before coronation could take place; his younger brother **Jayasimha II** ascended the throne. By the time his rule commenced, the kingdom was in difficulties.\n\n*   To avenge the humiliation caused to Munjaraja, Bhojaraja invaded the Chalukyan kingdom and occupied parts of it; inscriptions record that Bhoja conquered Karnataka, Lata and Konkan areas and held victory celebrations. But, contrary to Paramara inscriptions, Chalukyan inscriptions state that Jayasimha defeated the Paramaras and occupied the Northern areas of Kolhapur.\n    \n*   His commander-in-chief, **Chavanarasu**, also held the title \"Konkana dhoomaketa.\" Around C.E. 1020–24, conflicts between the Chalukya and Paramara dynasties reached an intensive form; Jayasimha personally led an expedition against the Paramara king. Chalukyan inscriptions state Saptamalava was conquered.\n    \n*   Jayasimha also faced war from the Cholas; Nolambavadi and Bellari areas were subjected to Chola attack, but inscriptions inform that all these areas were reoccupied. **Rajendra Chola defeated Jayasimha in the battle at Maski** and by C.E. 1026, occupied Rattapadi areas. As a result, the Chola war became serious; some say he began construction of the Kalyana fort in C.E. 1028.\n*   Jayasimha II had two wives — **Devalamahadevi** and **Suggaladevi** — who together, in C.E. 1029, donated Puligera area to Brahmarasi Pandita, a Pasupathacharya. Though he followed Jain religious principles in the beginning, he accepted **Kalamukha Saivism** due to the encouragement of these queens.\n*   His kingdom extended over the districts of Cuddapah and Anantapur in Rayalaseema, and the entire Telangana region. His titles: \"jagadekamalla,\" \"trailokamalla,\" \"vikramasimha.\"\n\n### Someswara I (1042 – 1068 A.D.)\n\nAfter Jayasimha II, his son Someswara I assumed power in C.E. 1042 and ruled up to C.E. 1068, with the titles \"aahavamalla,\" \"' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'rajanarayana\" and \"trailokamalla.\" He was the greatest among the Kalyani Chalukyas.\n\n*   **Bilhana**, in his _Vikramankadeva Charita_, mentions that Someswara built the **Kalyani city**, made it his capital and ruled from it. His chief queen, **Mylaladevi**, visited Srisailam in C.E. 1059 and donated gifts to Sureswara, a Jain scholar who was a Kalamukha Saiva guru.\n*   Nearly twelve fierce battles were fought with the Cholas even during his reign. By the time he assumed authority, he used to look after the affairs of the Potlakere region.\n*   After Someswara I ascended the throne, as Vijayaditya made one more effort to occupy Vengi, his help was sought; thinking Chola authority at Vengi could be removed, his army was sent. With this army, Vijayaditya defeated Rajaraja Narendra, drove him out, and occupied Vengi.\n*   His successful expedition went through Vengi, Kalinga, Panchala, Magadha, Malwa and Kerala kingdoms. Wars with Paramara rulers that had begun earlier continued during his time; the rulers of Konkan, Sevuna and Vindhya regions supported the Paramaras. His commander-in-chief **Nagavarma** participated in many wars, fought ably and achieved victories.\n*   **Kakatiyas came into prominence during the time of Someswara I.** In the expedition Someswara made on Konkan and Chakrakuta, **Prolaraju I** of the Kakatiya dynasty participated, and appreciating his services, was given control over Hanumakonda and honoured. Prola''s son, **Betaraju**, along with Someswara, participated in the wars fought with the Paramaras and Cholas.\n*   Because of Rajaraja''s statecraft, Chola influence in Vengi politics increased, leading to battles with the Western Chalukyas. After Rajaraja Narendra (C.E. 1022–61) came to the Vengi throne, Chola dominance in Vengi increased; conditions worsened, and internal conflicts took the stage. Vijayaditya, the half-brother of Rajaraja Narendra training to capture kingship, provided an opportunity for Western Chalukya entry into Vengi politics.\n\n**Continued conflict and death**\n\n*   Someswara I fought many battles with the Cholas while helping Vijayaditya, who resisted Rajarajanarendra and carried on his rule with Chola help and per Rajarajanarendra''s whims. The **Kalidindi inscription of Rajaraja Narendra** informs that three leading Chola commanders-in-chief were killed at the hands of Western Chalukyan armies. Even during Someswara''s time, battles were fought fiercely; his commanders-in-chief Gandapaiah and Gangadhara got killed in war at the hands of the Cholas, and Vijayaditya, Vikramaditya, Madhusudana, Ganapati and Angamaiah (commander-in-chief) all had to flee. With the Cholas'' rise, the Western Chalukyas suffered huge losses and several cities were destroyed.\n*   In the **Kopparam battle of C.E. 1053–54**, the Western Chalukyas killed Chola **Rajadhiraja**; Rajaraja Narendra, receiving no help from the Cholas under these circumstances, had no alternative except to accept the sovereignty of the Western Chalukyas.\n*   Someswara, who fought many battles — the Kanchi invasion (C.E. 1049), the Kopparam war (C.E. 1053–54), a raid on Vengi (C.E. 1061), and the Mudakkara battle (C.E. 1062) — had parts of his kingdom apportioned: his wife Mylaladevi was appointed supervisor over Banavasi region, son **Someswara II** was appointed over Belvala and Puligere areas, and another son **Vikramaditya VI** was appointed supervisor over Gangavadi region.\n*   The life of Someswara, who achieved many victories, ended tragically. In his last days, he was defeated in two wars fought at Kudali Sangamam. He asked the Cholas to come for battle the next day, but when the Chola armies arrived at the appointed time, Someswara did not come to the field. As he failed to appear, **Virarajendra occupied the kingdom**; unable to bear this final humiliation, and overtaken by a serious illness, **Someswara I, on 28th March C.E. 1068, went with a musical instrument to the Tungabhadra river and committed suicide by jumping into it.** Many inscriptions record his death, but Someswara I is remembered as the one ruler who got involved in incessant fighting with four emperors.\n\n### Someswara II (1068 – 1076 A.D.)\n\nAfter Someswara I, his son Someswara II carried on the rule from C.E. 1068 to 1076, with the title \"**bhuvanaikamalla**.\"\n\n*   Soon after his accession, Chola–Chalukya conflict started again. His brothers **Jayasimha** and **Vikramaditya VI** occupied Nolambavadi and Banavasi areas and rebelled against him. Chola **Virarajendra** invaded the Chalukyan kingdom; the Chalukyan cavalry force defeated the Chola armies and saved the Gutti fort.\n*   **Cause → Effect:** When Someswara kept his younger brother Vikramaditya VI in charge of protecting Gangavadi, the latter — having an eye on the Chalukyan throne — rebelled against his elder brother, defeated him, and, as stated in Bilhana''s _Vikramankadeva Charita_, occupied the kingdom.\n\n### Vikramaditya VI (1076 – 1126 A.D.)\n\nYounger brother of Someswara II and son of Someswara I; got coronated at Kalyani and ruled from C.E. 1076 to 1126 — considered a great king among the Western Chalukyas. His court poet **Bilhana''s** _Vikramankadeva Charita_ gives details of his reign.\n\n*   In the very first year of his reign, the **Salivahana era in vogue until then was changed and the Vikrama era was started in its place** — thus the **Chalukya Vikrama era** commenced from C.E. 1076.\n*   Though Kalyani remained his capital, to improve the popular system of administration, different towns such as Vijayapuram and Vikramapuram were developed as important sub-category towns. Frequently moving about in these towns, he maintained peace and had high popular respect; administration attracted popular favour.\n*   When his younger brother Jayasimha tried to rebel in C.E. 1080, it was suppressed. Aiming to occupy areas of the kingdom, the administrator of Vengi was defeated.\n*   During his time, **Vishnuvardhana** of the Hoyasala dynasty occupied some parts of the kingdom, passing through Uchchangi and Belvala areas; the Hoyasalas occupied territory up to the river Krishna. Taking advantage of this, administrators of other feudatory kingdoms tried to rebel.\n*   During his time, poets **Bilhana and Vijnaneswara** were patronised. Historians state that during his reign there was progress in all fields. He held the title \"tribhuvanamalla.\"\n\n### Someswara III (1126 – 1138 A.D.)\n\nAfter Vikramaditya VI, his son Someswara III ruled the kingdom from C.E. 1126 to 1138. He wrote the work _Abhilashitardhachintamani_, described as \"vijnanasarvasvam\" (encyclopedia), also called \"manasollasam.\"\n\nSoon after becoming king, he took Banavasi and Gangavadi regions from the feudatories into his own control. But during his time only, the Vengi kingdom defied the supremacy of the Kalyani Chalukyas.\n\n### Jagadekamalla (1138 – 1151 A.D.)\n\nDuring his time, the Hoyasalas rose in power. He was the son of Someswara III. After Jagadekamalla, his younger brother **Tailapa III (C.E. 1151–57)** assumed authority.\n\n### Decline and End of the Kalyani Chalukyas\n\nTailapa III''s reign period was filled with wars against feudatory kings. By that time, **Kalachuri Bijjala**, discharging the duties of a minister, had grown stronger and secured the cooperation of feudatory kingdoms.\n\n*   The minister of Bijjala was **Basaveswara**, the famous religious reformer; Bijjala favoured Jainism.\n*   **In C.E. 1157, Kalchuri Bijjala drove away Tailapa III**, who left the capital and fled.\n*   When the son of Tailapa III, **Someswara IV**, came to the capital Kalyani in C.E. 1183, **Ballala III**, the Hoyasala ruler, defeated him and drove him away. Someswara, for some time, lived in exile, and died at last in **C.E. 1200**.\n\n**With this, the Kalyani Chalukyan dynasty has ended.**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p9", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Administrative System", "bodyMd": "In the Chalukyan age, Coastal Andhra remained under Vengi, and integrated Western Andhra feudatory dynasties used to rule. These feudatories, while cooperating with their sovereigns in waging wars, began to rule their kingdoms independently; their courts resembled those of the emperor, and they maintained councils of ministers.\n\nRejecting considerations of country, caste and family, and becoming selfish, feudatories fought among themselves for the throne; among them, Bhimasaluki and Yuddhamalla did not hesitate even to mortgage the country''s independence. At that time armies roamed about freely; Vengi, Dharanikota, Nellore and Khammam got destroyed many times, and many villages were burnt — \"there is no exaggeration in stating that the entire country got turned into a big war camp.\"\n\nFrom another angle, some kingdoms flourished in a golden age (the Vemulavada Chalukyas). The administrative, economic and religious systems, social-life policy, architecture and sculpture, language and literature, and patronage of fine arts that developed during Chalukyan rule (sixth to twelfth century) in South India remained as **models for later dynasties** — the impact of these varied developments spread deeply into people''s lives and progress.\n\n### Emperor, Power, Status\n\nChalukyan government was a **hereditary monarchy**; the king was all in all. Kingly authority consisted of seven items (_saptanga_): king, minister, kingdom, treasury, army, friend and fort. In running the administration, the king was assisted by the **yuvaraja** (crown prince), also called **Uparaja**; kings without sons used to appoint their brothers or their brothers'' sons as yuvaraja.\n\nChalukyan kings used titles such as \"parameswara,\" \"paramabhattaraka,\" \"parama maheswara,\" \"prithvivallabha,\" \"Bhuvanasraya,\" \"Rajadhiraja\" and \"rajaparameswara.\" Though the emperor was all-powerful, custom required him to rule according to the **dharmasastras**. Though a gap existed in this age between the king and the people, the duties of the emperor consisted of:\n\n*   Protecting the people\n*   Solving disputes among the people and delivering justice\n*   Maintaining law and order in the country\n*   Saving the kingdom from external invasions\n\nFor discharging these duties, the emperor arranged the **mantrimandali** (council of ministers).\n\n### Council of Ministers\n\nFor helping the emperor in administration, the council of ministers was formed. It included those who knew the dharmasastras, the commanders-in-chief of the army, and important relatives of the king. The **minister, commander-in-chief, treasurer, purohit and yuvaraja** used to advise the king in administration.\n\n*   These posts were generally held by men who observed dharma, could be trusted, were of good character, and were brahmins well versed in the knowledge of dharmasastras. They held these posts **hereditarily**.\n*   During the Chalukyan times, these posts were gradually also held by Sudras. It is also learnt that these posts were held, as per ancient moral codes, by the \"eighteen tirthas\" of Sapthangas.\n*   In the Vemulavadi inscription of Arikesari II, employees known as \"mahasandhivigraha,\" \"tantrapala\" and \"Satradipala\" are mentioned. Employees referred to as \"niyogadikrutha\" and \"niyogavallabha\" perhaps served as supervisors of departments. It could be said that the **bahattara (72) niyogadhipaties** found in Kakatiya inscriptions might have been the descendants of these earlier officials. These employees received land grants as honorarium for their services.\n\n### Local Government\n\nChalukyan administration to some extent resembled that of the Cholas.\n\n**Administrative divisions:**\n\n| Unit | Head |\n|---|---|\n| Rashtra (province) | Rashtradhipati |\n| Vishaya (also called Nadu) | Vishyadhipati |\n| Village | Gramani |\n\n*   For administrative convenience, the empire was divided into provinces, and provinces were divided into **vishayas** (also called _nadus_): Kammanadu, Prolanadu, Attilinadu and Venginadu were among the names of local government units. In Rayalaseema, names like Seemanadu are found; in Southern Andhra, rashtras were called \"Kottalu\" — mentioned in the inscriptions of Pulinadu, located as Perumbagapadi.\n*   It is learnt from inscriptions that there were **thirty vishayas in Venginadu**.\n*   Local government was, to a great extent, in the hands of feudatory kings. In **Yellamanchilli, Pithapuram and Mudigonda**, only kings of Chalukyan dynasties assumed authority; besides them, the **Pattavardhani, Durjaya and Haihayadi** feudatory dynasties also rose.\n*   **Village was the foundation of the administrative system.** A Grama sabha used to be there in every village; the village system attained a stable shape during the time of the Eastern Chalukyas. **Gramikas** were the important village officers during the time of the Rashtrakutas.\n*   The Machilipatnam inscription of Chalukya Bhima II informs that village administration was carried out through committees; important decisions in village affairs used to be taken in the presence of village officers. Even emperors, when making land grants in villages, used to take their permission. Grama sabhas existed in Renadu; during their time, **Karanams** used to maintain village accounts. It is learnt through inscriptions that in Kunganoor, a person named Adavallam was appointed Karanam by the Grama sabha.\n\n### Kingdom''s Income and Expenditure\n\n**Sources of income**\n\n*   Tribute paid by feudatories to the kings was the main source of income for the Chalukyan government.\n*   Some villages decided a yearly tax for the entire village rather than individual taxes — a method known as **\"gramvar.\"**\n*   Along with land tax, several other kinds of taxes existed; taxes could be paid either in cash or in kind. **Land tax amounted to 1/6th of the produce.** The word \"sarvakarapariharam,\" used at the time of granting agraharas, indicates the wider tax net.\n*   During the reign of **Trinetrudu**, king of Vaidumba, in Renadu, it is recorded that taxes on farmers such as _degaracha_ tax, _padevalu pannu_, _padiyeri pannu_ and _Sandhivigraha_ tax were removed.\n*   Taxes such as _Kallavakkam, Vidanali Vishaya Sunkam, biradayam_ and _chittavatam_ are seen in Rayalaseema inscriptions.\n*   The **Koravi inscription** issued by Niravadya, the Mudigonda Chalukyan king (C.E. 935), records that in Telangana, taxes were collected by kings from village leaders as a fixed or contractual amount; fines levied on criminals and other miscellaneous taxes could be collected by the village leader.\n*   The **Vaidumba \"Irugaya maharaju\" inscription** (Vayalpadu Taluka, Chittoor district) records that village leaders such as _rattodi_ and _rattagudi_ could pay the king taxes such as _dogaraja_ tax, _padevali_ tax, _padiyeri_ tax and _sandhivigraha_ tax, while other miscellaneous taxes could be taken by the leaders themselves — ' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '_dogaraja_ tax was for the maintenance of the yuvaraja; _padevali_ tax was collected for maintaining the army; _sandhivigraha_ tax was for protecting the kingdom from enemy attack.\n*   The Chevuru copper inscription of Eastern Chalukyan ruler Ammaraja I (C.E. 935) informs that, while appointing a village official for Mumikili village, it was laid down that eight _gadyanas_ paid for the endowment of the _gramani_ had to be paid to the king only, and the remaining miscellaneous taxes need not be paid by the gramani.\n*   Employees decided the additional tax to be paid during the crop season, depending on the produce; **Rachakaranas** and **Rachasettis** collected this. Professional taxes were also levied — potters, washermen, barbers, goldsmiths and blacksmiths had to pay professional taxes. Chalukyas also received revenue from taxes on water-drawing machines, house tax, oil-mill tax, cattle tax, sheep tax, and market tax.\n\n**Expenditure**\n\n*   Chalukyan kings spent income from taxes on the army, royal family maintenance, salaries and allowances of employees, welfare activities, and charities and philanthropy.\n*   It is learnt from Sukra''s _Neetisaram_ that a king always had to keep **1/6th** of income in the treasury; of the remaining income, **half was to be spent on the army**, **1/12th on royal family expenses**, **another 1/12th on salaries and allowances of employees**, **another 1/12th on people''s welfare**, and the **last 1/12th on charities and philanthropy**.\n*   Public welfare activities of the time included: construction and maintenance of temples, patronage of poets/scholars/artists, development of educational activities, construction and maintenance of irrigation tanks, donations and charities, performance of sacrifices and rituals, and donations to religious institutions and mattahas.\n\n### Judicial Administration\n\nClear information is not known about the judicial organisation followed by the Chalukyan rulers, though some inscriptions give references.\n\n*   **The king remained the chief justice of the kingdom.** Judgments were given by \"rashtradhipati\" in provinces and \"vishayapati\" in vishayas; in villages, Grama sabhas gave judgments. Ordinary disputes were decided entirely in gramasabhas.\n*   There were special judges called **\"pradvivakas.\"** When disputes occurred between villages over land borders or use of water from a river, kings had them resolved through the pradvivakas, their ministers, or caste elders. These courts followed principles recorded in ancient Smriti works such as the **Yajnavalkya Smriti**.\n*   Judgments delivered by these courts were issued under the king''s emblem and in the name of \"jayapatras.\" Special officers called **\"mudravartulas\"** were responsible for stamping the emblem.\n*   The **Koravi copper-plate inscription (C.E. 935)**, considered the first Telugu inscription, gives details of the criminal investigation procedure followed under the Chalukyas. **\"Mitakshara\" of Vijnaneswara** and **\"Neetikavyamruta\" of Somadevasuri** are referred to as the dharmasastra works of the age. **\"Smritisangraha,\"** otherwise known as \"Saraswathi Vilasam,\" written by \"Prataparudragajapati\" (a contemporary of the Yelamanchili Chalukya family), also informs about the justice and judicial codes of the Chalukyan times.\n*   Only kings had the right to protect dharma and punish criminals; though the king was at the apex of the judicial system, in many parts of the kingdom he established courts and appointed judges. According to the Kollipara copper-plate inscription, **Arikesari I**, as a judge, established a court named **\"dharma sabha.\"** According to \"Saraswati Vilasam,\" members of the dharmasabha, numbering 3, 5 or 7, knowing the Vedas and judicial codes, were ordained to be truthful and impartial. Membership was denied to those with no knowledge of the kingdom''s traditions and sastras, and to those known for cruelty.\n*   Even guilds had special courts. As stated in \"Vaisya purana,\" opposing the wrong decisions of king \"Rajamahendravara,\" the vaisyas of 18 towns got organised into a **\"vaisya sangha.\"**\n*   Responsibility for preventing village thefts belonged to a village official called **Talari** — the leading servant of the village, also called village Police, pattilaka, pattera and Patel. His duty was to catch the thief and hand him over to the concerned legal authority; if he failed to catch the thief, he had to pay the cost of the stolen articles himself. This method was known as **\"atchukavali.\"**\n*   During Chalukyan times, **death sentence was the highest punishment**. At some places, though the intensity of punishment to brahmins was lessened somewhat, they never escaped punishment entirely. Depending on the degree of offence, punishments included: cutting of hands and legs, cutting of ears and nose, cutting the fore-part of the fingers of hands and legs, and burning the body in flames.\n*   If a court employee committed a wrong, he was removed from post and his sources of income lessened — such an incident occurred in Pabubarru village, donated by Saktivarma, when a gramani who failed to obey the king''s orders was removed from post. Basing on this, the Mudigonda and Vengi Chalukyas followed the codes of Saktivarma: if a person killed another, **120 drammas** was levied as penalty; if a person caused physical harm to another, **60 drammas** was imposed. If the modesty of a lady was attacked, a fine of **25 drammas** was imposed. As per the Srimadhavigeya maharaja inscription of Vaidumba king Bhuvanatrinetra, in cases of sexual atrocities, punishment was imposed on both men and women — males were fined **64 gadyanas**, and for women, **the nose used to be cut**. On the whole, punishments remained hard.\n\n### Military Organisation\n\nChalukyan emperors used to personally lead their armies during wars, even while maintaining well-trained commanders-in-chief. **Kshatriyas** became famous as soldiers of courage and bravery. Because of constant Chalukya–Pallava–Chola–Rashtrakuta–Kalyani Chalukya conflicts, the need for soldiers increased, and persons of courage and bravery from **all castes** were taken and appointed as soldiers and commanders-in-chief.\n\n*   Because of wars, a greater portion of the income received (from taxes and war loot) was spent on army maintenance. Chalukyan feudatory chiefs used to come, as and when needed, with their own armies to wage wars for the protection of the Chalukyan kingdom — hence it was said that the **main strength of the Eastern Chalukyan kingdom lay in the forces of their feudatory chiefs**.\n*   Foot soldiers, elephant force and naval force were the main organs of the army. The **Aihole inscription of Pulakesi II** gives details of **six types of army organisations**:\n    1.  **Mula** — the hereditary force\n    2.  **Bhritya** — those who accompanied soldiers\n    3.  **Sreni** — guild-based feudatory forces\n    4.  **Mitra** — armies of friendly kingdoms\n    5.  **Atavika** — forces of forest-based tribes\n    6.  **Amitra** — captured forces\n*   Army employees were called **\"mahabaladhikruta\"** (Great Commander-in-Chief). Forts were under the control of the **\"durgadhipathi,\"** who bore full responsibility for the protection of the fort — some army units were under his control. For army maintenance, either the wealth of the emperor or the income from villages was allotted.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p10", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Economic Development", "bodyMd": "### Agriculture, Irrigation, Crops, Land Revenue, Other Taxes\n\nDuring the Chalukyan period, people''s dependence on land and agriculture increased — a development that had begun after the Satavahanas. Brahmins, associated with temple service, were given lands and agraharas and turned into landlords; Sudras cultivated their lands and remained loyal to them. As pressure on land grew, expansion of agriculture became inevitable, and uncultivated and forest lands were converted into crop lands.\n\n*   For tax purposes, agricultural lands were divided into different types: **low-lying/wet lands, dry lands, sandy lands (garuvu) and garden lands.** Land tax was fixed depending on water facility, land fertility and crop yield.\n*   Chalukyan-era inscriptions reference commercial crops such as coconut, betel leaf, betel nut, mango, sugarcane, cotton, grams and pulses.\n\n**Irrigation**\n\n*   Emperors took steps to construct tanks and wells, and dig canals to pool water resources for agriculture. Rich people, feudatories and wealthy farmers helped construct and maintain tanks.\n*   The **Chaita tank** and **Bhimasamudram** tank, mentioned in the Tadikonda inscription of Ammaraju II, still supply water to cultivated lands today.\n*   A Western Chalukya inscription mentions a big tank called **Ananta Sarovaram** in Chebrolu (Guntur district). A Mahaboobnagar district inscription mentions a tank called **Bhimasamudram**. A big tank existed in Nalgonda district, under which 12 nivartanas of land were donated to brahmins. In Medak district, a tank known as **\"madivojanakare\"** existed. Feudatories of Kullottunga Rajendra II dug the **Polinayaka** and **Potta** tanks.\n*   For those who brought new lands under cultivation, tax exemption was given in the beginning for some years, encouraging agriculture. Garden lands and light-soil lands were donated to temples — e.g., for _akhanda deepa_ maintenance at the Bheemeswara temple, Draksharamam, a garden of 2000 betel-nut trees was donated; a mango garden for the deity in Penta village (Nizamabad district) and one more mango garden for the temple at Bapatla were also donated.\n\n**Land rights and taxes**\n\n*   Every landlord had full authority over his land for donation, buying and selling, though acceptance from concerned village elders had to be obtained before taking these up. Nobody could hold personal rights over **banjar land**.\n*   When land was donated, the extent of tax exemption given was known through **\"sarvakara Pariharam\"** — meaning exempted from all taxes.\n*   On occasion, when kings and feudatories made donations depending on need, there was a custom of buying private individuals'' lands and giving them to the donee.\n*   Land tax related terms: **Ari** — tax paid after change of crop; **Siddhayam** — tax that had already been levied whether or not the crop came up; **taggu, tere** — other agriculture-related taxes.\n*   Grain measurements in use: nivartana, martu, khandika, putti; even today in Telangana, measures such as putted manika and sola exist. For land measurement, there were \"gedalu\" (possibly wooden sticks) known as rajamana, mummidibhimakola, Janakola and rajarajakola.\n*   Crop conditions, types and rain relationships were explained in a work known as **\"sasyananda.\"** Writers such as **Somadevasuri** opined that taxes should not be collected before crops ripened and armies should not be quartered on crop lands — failing this would destroy the kingdom.\n*   **King Vijayaditya''s tax schedule** for lands in Malliswaradeva town (Vengi mandalam):\n\n| Land Type | Tax (per putti) |\n|---|---|\n| Oodpu paddy field | 2 chinnas |\n| Oodpu field | 6 chinnas |\n| Dry land | 4 chinnas |\n| Jowar land | (damaged in inscription — unknown) |\n| New land | 2 chinnas |\n| Gingelly land | (not preserved in inscription) |\n\nBasing on such schedules and land productivity, tax used to be fixed; concessions were given for newly-cultivated lands, and animal husbandry was also encouraged.\n\n### Industries\n\nChalukyan-era inscriptions show industries, trade and commerce were in good condition — industries allied to agriculture, jewellery-making, vehicle- and object-manufacturing, and smaller craft-based industries all existed.\n\n*   Most agricultural-era wealth was spent on wars, luxuries and pomp; only a smaller portion went to production and welfare programmes. The administration settled craftsmen in villages by giving craft land grants and tax exemptions — villagers manufactured land-tilling implements (arakalu), cots, benches and low-seat armless single-seaters (peetalu). Iron ploughs and earthenware pottery were also made.\n*   An inscription from the Draksharamam area shows **Teliki** (oil-extractor), **potterer, blacksmith, carpenter, maker of brass utensils (kanchari), and basket maker (medari)** were given tax-free lands.\n*   Cloth industry continued through the period; thin, delicate fabrics were made for emperors and queens, while thicker woven cloth served common people. Implements and vessels were made with great skill in all metals, from gold to iron. Most villages had oil presses (ganugulu) and weaving looms.\n\n### Trade and Commerce\n\nTrade and commerce flourished during the Chalukyan age, though industry remained largely confined to rural needs. Powerful trade guilds carried on brisk trade, mostly in weapons for emperors and armies, luxury articles for the wealthy, spices, perfumes, diamonds, pearls, crystals, precious stones, and finely textured cloth.\n\n*   Barter continued at a high level in villages, while coins circulated in towns. **Powerful merchant guilds** emerged; inscriptions record that vaisyas, telula (oil-pressers), perikas and balejas had their own guilds. Huge wealth came to the government through these guilds in the form of taxes and levies.\n*   Fairs and pilgrimage places developed into trade centres — the fair at **Konidesa** used to run every Thursday.\n*   Business was mainly in the hands of the **vaisyas**, called _nakarams_; every nakara had a _sreshti_. **Penugonda** became a leading trade city with 18 leading cities in the Vengi kingdom as central points. **Vengi, Ghantasala, Visakhanagaram and Nidadavolu** were trade centres. The **Ahadanakara inscription** informs about trade-guild activities. **Prithvivallabhapatnam** was considered a leading port town of the time.\n*   The presence of Eastern Chalukyan coins in **Burma and Siam** testifies to continental trade. Many Andhra port towns were given Tamil names during this period: **Visakhapatnam** was named **Kulottunga Cholapattanam**, **Ghantasala** was named **Chola Pandyapuram**, and **Motupalli** was named **Deseeyakonda pattanam**.\n\n**Coinage**\n\n*   Currency in usage: **gadyana, mada, panam, kalamju, dramas** — denoting gold, silver and copper coins.\n*   As per **Pavuluri Mallanna''s** work, the relations between different coins were:\n\n| Conversion |\n|---|\n| 4 gokara kanulu = 1 sorakani |\n| 4 sora kanulu = 1 pittha |\n| 4 pitthalu = 1 kani |\n| 4 kanulu = 1 visam |\n| 4 visalu = 1 pattika |\n| 4 pattikalu = 1 chinnam |\n| 4 chinnalu = 1 madha |\n\n*   Besides coins, liquid measures (sola, manika, adde, kuncham) and solid measures (tulam, seru, veese, manugu, putti) were also in use.\n*   **Trade guilds never sold all goods** — each guild sold only one commodity; those sel' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'ling a particular commodity formed themselves into a guild, and only they could sell that commodity. On goods brought for sale, name-duty and place-duty were imposed; tax existed on salt and on cattle sold, collected only by officials. In the name of \"Kolpuveesalu,\" a **Kolagaram tax** existed; when grain and other goods were measured, a duty was taken out of the total measured.\n*   **Vedic religion did not allow sea voyage**, so Chalukyas did not carry on much foreign trade directly. Goods came in Chinese ships from Eastern countries and East Indies, and goods of the time were carried back in the same ships. Eastern Chalukyas sent ambassadors to Burma and China and maintained friendly relations with them, though foreign trade remained limited. Some locally-grown commodities and manufactured cloth were exported; horses and spices needed for emperors were imported from foreign countries. **Pavuluri Mallanna**, of the 12th century, gave the prices of commodities in his work \"Sarasangrahaganitam.\"\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p11", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Social Development", "bodyMd": "To know about social conditions of the Chalukyan period, their inscriptions prove useful. **Vedic Brahman religion gained prominence and the varna system took stable shape.** Protection of _varnasrama dharma_ went on well during their rule.\n\n*   Migrations as per occupation, regional bases, and the influence of different religions caused divisions to crop up in many castes. At this stage, there was **social uplift** in the lifestyle of people at the lowest rungs of the social ladder. From this age onward, people had freedom to make charities as per their desire. **Sanskrit education**, unlike earlier times, was made open to all from this age. Each caste''s people had freedom to make necessary alternatives or additions to their caste regulations. Social customs and festivals varied from caste to caste — inscriptions giving information regarding the honour and status of different castes are seen in this period, though details on marriage relations and inter-dining between different castes and varnas are not clearly known.\n\n### Brahmins\n\nIn the Chalukyan four-fold varna system, **brahmins held first place**, remaining at the top of society because of their Sanskrit education, study of Vedas and Vedangas, and service as teachers.\n\n*   Reference to them is found in all royal-dynasty inscriptions; many emperors gave them agraharas — a system personally started by **Pulakesi II**. In Chalukyan age, all the ministers and dandanayakas who stood by the ruling dynasties belonged to the brahmin community only. Depending on gotra and pravara branch, there were divisions even among brahmins, identified as per the areas or centres in which brahmin families resided.\n*   Due to discontent established in religious and political fields, royal patronage, and personal or family difficulties, brahmin families since the **9th century A.D.** started migrating; rulers provided arrangements for their stay. During Chalukyan times, in the Ghantasala area, house sites were given to **2000 brahmin families**.\n*   More than their supposed eminence, their place in society came to be recognised because of their respected profession; countless agraharas are referenced in inscriptions given for maintaining items related to Vedic education and their livelihood.\n\n### Kshatriyas\n\nMany battles took place during the Vengi Chalukyan period, needing many soldiers for wars. **These kshatriyas went as soldiers at the time of wars and fought.** Those kshatriyas who considered administrative duties their responsibility came to be described as of \"warrior varna.\"\n\n*   From the Chalukyan time onward, **all categories of people began to participate in all kinds of activities**. To claim equal status with the old kshatriyas, every caste''s people, from Chalukyan times, began to derive their origin from some pristine Purana Personality, and following this, as stated in inscriptions, members began to claim themselves of **lunar dynasty (Chandravamsa)** and **Surya vamsa (solar dynasty)**. The idea was prevalent that whoever performed kshatriya dharma or duty was kshatriya alone.\n\n### Vysyas\n\nIn the Four-fold varna organisation, vaisyas formed the third group; their occupation was trade. They were called **sreshti, setti and komati**. Being devotees of Gomateswara (Jainism), they were also called _gomatlu_ or _komatlu_.\n\n*   During Chalukyan times, trade and business were their occupation; organising into guilds and carrying on business both within and outside the country, they became rich. The fact that vaisyas could have their own armies for protecting their properties and providing security during trade journeys, and even giving loans to the administration itself, indicates their economic growth.\n*   Vaisyas also had **born gotra names**. For their rights, they formed \"**nakaram**\" (nagaram); guilds worked to solve caste-related problems. Their caste goddess was **Vasavikanyaka** at Penugonda — there is no truth in the story mentioned in their \"Kula puranam\" that when a Chalukyan ruler tried to force her, she jumped into fire and died.\n*   More than following administrators'' orders, observing caste dharma and taking permission from caste elders for social activities was the prevailing norm.\n\n### Sudras\n\nThose belonging to the fourth group in the four-fold varna system came to be regarded as sudras. Just like other castes, many divisions emerged in their caste too, depending on occupation adopted for living, birth and regionalism — some took to agriculture, others adopted handicrafts.\n\n*   During the Medieval age, many of those who took to military service happened to be sudra varna; their involvement was there in industries, handicrafts, and temple activities.\n*   During Chalukyan times, opportunity came for some sudras to assume authority over the kingdom — Chalukya feudatories such as **Velanati kings, Kondapadamatlu, Tyagi and Parichchedi** all belonged to sudra caste only.\n*   Contemporary historians state that people of craft-based occupations craved for social status; with the expansion of agriculture, their economic condition also gradually improved. The divisions among the sudras — **Velama and Kamma** — came as per regional divisions (Velanadu, Kammanadu); **Reddy**, in the first stage, was either a Rashtrakuta chief or Rattodi (an official of village administration) and gradually remained as the name of a division among the sudras.\n*   As per Chalukyan-age inscriptions, in Edurupalli village near Draksharama, land grants were given separately to **teliki (oil-pressers), kammari (blacksmith), kamsali (carpenter), kanchari (makers of bronze articles)**, coconut and betel-nut gardeners, barbers, bronze workers, washermen, basket makers, and leather workers.\n*   It is learnt from the organisation \"sanimunnurvuru\" that, just like people of other castes and occupations, **prostitutes also got organised into guilds** and led a movement for the protection of their rights, referred to in many inscriptions.\n\n### Position of Women\n\nIn the patriarchal form of society prevalent during Chalukyan times, there was restriction on women, but they were \"no impediment for the honour and status of women.\"\n\n*   Though Smriti works and Jain works imposed restrictions and limitations on the freedoms of women, this need not be taken as reflecting contemporary realities — from politics to fine arts, there are many sources mentioning women''s interference and participation.\n*   Though marriage alliances did not generally occur between the Chalukya–Rashtrakuta dynasties, on other occasions, as **Chola princesses in succession married Chalukyan kings**, significant changes occurred in history. Because of queens such as **Kundavva, Ammangadevi and Madhurantaki**, it could be thought that Chola influence got established over the Vengi monarchy.\n*   **Upper caste ladies and prostitutes used to earn skill in fine arts and other needed types of education.**\n\n### Educational Organisation\n\nIn the Chalukyan age, religion-related Vedic studies received a lot of patronage. **Puranas, itihasas, dharmasastras, agamasastras, grammar, Vedanta, Astronomy, Diplomacy, Mathematics, Ayurveda and Logic** were studied.\n\n*   In land-grant inscriptions, the rich knowledge of brahmin grant-receivers has been greatly described — in the Peddamaddali inscription, it is said that **Gupta Sarma** studied seventy commentaries on Logic alone. The Nidaparru inscription states that **Katte Sarma** thoroughly studied Vedas, itihasas, Puranas, dharmasastras and Upanishads; his grandfather, **Mandasarma**, was a scholar in Kalpa, purana, itihasa and dharmasastras at **Asanapura Ghatikasthana**.\n*   Asanapuram became famous as a prominent **Ghatikasthanam** — the most eminent educational centre was called Ghatikasthanam. During Chalukyan times, **Ayurvedam, Dharmavedam, Gandharva and Arthasastras** used to be taught. Those who graduated from such Ghatikas were called **Ghatikasikas**.\n*   **Rajaraja Narendra** stated he had studied Puranas, dharmasastras, kavyas and dramas, and had taken interest in Eashwaragamala. Educational learning mainly took place at **Agraharas, Brahmapurillu, Ghatikas, temples and matthas.** For traditional Sanskrit educational progress, agraharas remained as abodes given to scholars for patronising education and culture; sources state education was taught freely in agraharas, temples and matthas, and boarding/lodging facilities were provided.\n*   **Nolambeswara temple in Hemavati** was a Ghatikasthanam; the **Hiresamudra inscription** mentions boarding arrangements provided for more than ninety students. Some educational centres had libraries — at **Nagai**, a big library was run, attached to an educational centre.\n*   The educational system received financial help from the government, as well as cooperation and support from individuals, institutions and guilds. Some temples ran educational choultries.\n*   Great effort was made in Mathematics during this period — **Pavuluri Mallanna**, who lived during Rajaraja Narendra''s time, translated **\"Ganitasastram\"** written by **Mahaviracharya** (a Jain scholar), with the name **\"Sarasangraha ganitam.\"** This work mentions the coins, tables and measurements in use at that time.\n\n* * *", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p12", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Cultural Development", "bodyMd": "### Religious Developments\n\nAmong those who received Agraharas from the Chalukyan kings, brahmins — the basic pillars of Vedic religion — were greatest in number. During Chalukyan times, **Vedic religion was in the highest position**; **Kumarilabhattu**, who propagated _purvamimamsa Vedanta_ method, belonged to this age.\n\n*   Because of self-reforms within Vedic religion, people''s interest towards rituals and charities, idol worship, and construction of major temples increased. **Temple construction was taken up by the government as a movement and a social necessity.**\n*   It became a tradition that the number of wars fought by Chalukyan kingdoms had to be equalled by the construction of an equal number of temples: **Vijayaditya II**, who waged **108 wars**, built **108 temples**; **Chalukya Bhima**, who waged **360 wars**, built **360 temples**.\n*   The temple group at **Bikkavolu** was constructed during the time of Gunaga Vijayaditya; **Pancharamas**, prominent Saiva temples, were constructed during the time of the Chalukyas. A huge number of temples were built during the time of **Rajaraja Narendra**.\n*   **Jataras** used to be held in temples — the _pradigam_ (procession) of idols was an important aspect. The Bezawada inscription (C.E. 930) of Yuddhamalla records that in Chebrolu, Mahasena''s jatara procession was taken from Chebrolu to Bezawada.\n\n### Saivism\n\nSaivism had popular following during this period. In Saiva deeksha, **rudraksha, vibhuti and gurupadakam** (water from the feet of the teacher) formed main aspects; differences existed among Saiva branches regarding the manner of deeksha. A 7th-century inscription refers to \"sivamandala deeksha.\"\n\n*   At places like Vijayawada, propaganda known as \"simha parishats\" was established. **Srisailam, Alampuram and Draksharamam** had been exclusively Saiva centres; **Srisailam has been described by Bhavabhuti as a Kapalika centre.**\n*   The temples built in the almost early stages of Chalukyan rule were all related to Saivism only. Siva was described as \"mahavrati\" by Nannechoda; Kapalika _vratam_ was only mahavratam. **Srisailam, Alampuram, Bezawada (Vijayawada) and Pithapuram** were the centres of Sakti worship of the day.\n*   During this age, **Basaveswara (C.E. 1100–70)** in Karnataka started the **Vira Saiva movement**. Already before him, **Ekanta Ramaiah** and **Sripati Pandita** had propagated Virasaiva doctrines. It is said that, to prove the superiority of Siva, Ekanta Ramaiah got his head cut and received it back; Sripati Pandita (Vijayawada) kept fire in his upper garment (\"uttariyam\") to exhibit the superiority of Siva.\n*   The idea that Siva was supreme deity, the wearing of _linga_ as an instrument of devotion, and the integration of _linga_ (idol) and _anga_ (body) had been chief aspects of Virasaivism, which was called \"agamantam\" (end of agama). After Basaveswara, **Mallikarjuna Pandita** propagated Saivism further — while going around the country, he exterminated Jains and Buddhists and propagated Saivism.\n*   **Ammaraju II** donated Tadikonda village to Bhuvanasraya Siva temple in Vijayawada — on behalf of the Siva temple, this land grant was received by **Prabhutarasi**, a Saiva saint. Vemulavada Chalukyas also gave donations to Saiva saints; among them, **Arikesari I** donated Belmoga village to Sadyosivacharya as an educational grant.\n\n### Vaishnavism\n\nWith the new vigour that came from the propagation of **Ramanuja**, Vaishnavism began to compete with Saivism. In the Chalukya–Chola age, there were many Dravida Vaishnava families. \"**Keyurabahu Charithra**\" informs that **Kommana** minister carried out Vishnu installation in **32 temple centres**. In Vaishnava deeksha, \"Chakrankitalu,\" \"Pundradharana,\" \"Uchishista Bhojana,\" \"dasanamadharana\" and \"ekadasivratam\" had been main aspects.\n\n*   During Chalukyan age, extremist movements sprang up for the sake of Saiva and Vaishnava faiths. **Ramanujacharya** imparted Vaishnava deeksha to lower-varna people but did not condemn the varna system. **Palanati Brahmanayudu** propagated Vira Vaishnavism.\n*   Besides Virasaiva and Viravaishnava religions, over and above sectarian differences, the traditional **Smarta faith** also received encouragement. Vaishnava temples such as **Bapatla Bhavanaryana Swami temple, Sa' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'rpavarapu Bhavanarayanaswami Temple and Mandvya Narayanaswamy temple at Samarlakota** attained prominence during the Chalukyan time.\n\n### Buddhism\n\nIt is learnt through the biography of Panditaradhya that Buddhists lived in Chandole area. From the sentence \"Chaityamatynatam yatra nana chitrasuchitatvam,\" found in Amaravati inscriptions, it is understood that the **Dhanyakataka Chaitya did not suffer loss**, but Buddha, as an incarnation of Vishnu only, was getting worshipped — this is also stated in inscriptions at Amaravati, Karimnagar and Bikkavolu.\n\nThe name-sake Buddha worship, as a result of extremist Saiva propaganda by Panditaradhya, got ended; after the 5th and 6th centuries C.E., Buddhism began declining in the region.\n\n### Jainism\n\nInscriptional sources record that **Jainism was patronised** during the time of the Badami, Vengi and Kalyani Chalukyas.\n\n*   **Ayyanamahadevi**, queen of Kubjavishnuvardhana, established **Nedumba Vasadi** at Bezawada and donated Mushinikonda village to it.\n*   **Ammaraju II**, at the request of his wife \"Chamakamba,\" donated \"Kalachumbarru\" village to the Jains; at the request of **Durgaraju**, head of Karmarashtra vishaya, he gave many donations for constructing Katakabharana Jina temples. Further, Ammaraju II, at the request of his commanders-in-chief **Bhima and Naravahana**, gave Pedagadelaparru village for constructing Jina temples at Bezawada.\n*   **Ramathirtham, Bikkavolu, Hanumakonda, Potlacheruvu, Kolanupaka (Nalgonda) and Vardhamanapuram** were prominent Jain centres. **Vemulavada**, the capital of the Vemulavada Chalukyas (feudatories of the Rashtrakutas ruling in present Telangana), and their early capital, **Podanapuram (Bodhan)**, were also leading Jain centres — at **Potlacheruvu (Patan Cheruvu)** alone, there were around **500 Jain Vasadis** (places of stay for Jain monks).\n*   **Vimaladitya**, the Vengi Chalukya king, also patronised Jainism, adopting it toward the end of his rule. During the time of **Anantavarma Rajarajadeva**, who ruled Kalinga in C.E. 1178, a merchant named Kannamanayaka built the Rajaraja Jina temple at Bhogaram village (Visakhapatnam district) and made land grants to it. **Danavulapadu** in Jammalamadugu Taluk developed as a great Jain temple centre.\n*   During Vemulavada Chalukya times, **Yuddhamalla Jina temple at Sanigaram, Baddega Jina temple at Vemulavada (Subhadama jina temple), and Arikesari Jina temple at Repaka** were constructed. At Bodhan, it seems there was earlier a statue of Gomattheswara; seeing this, **Chamundaraya**, minister of Rachamalla Gangaraju, installed the idol of Gomattheswara at **Sravanabelagola** in Karnataka around C.E. 970–990.\n*   **Somadevasuri**, a Jainacharya with the Vemulavada kings, was a great scholar who wrote **\"Yasastilaka\"** — a champu kavya, also called **Yasodhara Charitra** — and **\"Neetikavyamrutam.\"** For him, king Baddega built a famous Jain temple, the **Subhadama Jina temple**, at Vemulavada; for its maintenance, **Arikesari III** donated the village **Vanikatupalli**.\n*   **Pampa**, the poet, in his \"Vikramarjuna Vijayam,\" also gave an account of Jain Tirthankaras. **Jinavallabha**, brother of Pampa, was himself a Jain devotee — he issued an inscription stating he had the statue of the Jain goddess **Chakreswari** carved on a hillock near Kurkyala village (about 20 km from Karimnagar) and held celebrations there.\n*   **Hanumakonda** is one of the ancient Jain temple centres in Telangana. On the present-day **Padmavathi hillock**, images of the Jain Tirthankaras Parsvanatha and Sasanadevi are found. In Jain Sangha, both _yathulu_ (monks) and _upasakulu_ (laity) existed; among Jains, divisions such as **Sangha, Gana, Gachha, Bali and Anya** existed. Educational learning of Jains started with the principle \"Om namah Siddhamnamah\" — it is the opinion of some that, by adding \"Sivayanamah,\" this was later turned into a Jain principle.\n\n### Literary Development\n\n**Telugu language**, which till then remained \"like a forest tree in unknown state,\" started from the time of the Chalukyas to develop into a tree blossoming with leaves, flowers and fruits. The great literary movement that started in this age went on progressing; most Telugu inscriptions of the Chalukya–Chola age are in verse form, and every verse inscription turned out to be a masterpiece.\n\n*   As per the **Chebrolu inscription**, the nature of literary industry and the fact that in those days _marga_ and _desi_ literary styles were prevalent in Telugu are known. **Nannechoda** was the greatest prevalent poet of the age; as far as known in Telugu, **he was the first poet honoured by the royal dynasty**, holding the title \"Kavirajasekhara\" for his \"Kumarasambhavam.\"\n*   The influence of Kannada language and literary tradition was high; **Prakrit** remained the state language during Satavahana and Ikshvaku times, and from the time of the Vishnukundins, **Sanskrit** became the state language, with Telugu remaining only the medium of the common people''s matters. **For the first time, it was the Eastern Chalukyas who gave the honour of official language to Telugu.**\n*   Though Sanskrit inscriptions were more numerous during their period, the Chalukyas also issued inscriptions in Telugu. The **Telugu Chola** rulers, ruling the Renadu area, were the first to introduce the practice of getting royal inscriptions written in Telugu; the **Vipparla inscription of Jayasimhavallabha I** was the **first Eastern Chalukyan inscription** in Telugu. **Mallikarjuna Panditaradhya** wrote \"Sivatatvasaram\" — more than 400 kanda verses — recognised by scholars as a **Sataka**; hence, the credit for starting the \"Sataka Prakriya\" (style) in Telugu goes to Panditaradhya.\n*   Among those who used the Telugu language were the **Renati Cholas, Western Chalukyas and their feudatories, and Bana kings**. In inscriptional Telugu of the first stage, all inscriptions were written in prose, and Prakrit words got mixed into Telugu extensively — e.g. the word \"reddi\" appeared as \"rattagudi/rattadi/rattodi\"; \"purugulu\" for \"nasanam\" (destroyer) appeared as \"pruvullu\"; \"cheyuvadu\" (rachuvasu) used to appear; plural indicators used \"lu\"; \"gudulu\" and \"madulu\" were called \"gudlu\" and \"madlu\"; \"padamata\" was uttered as \"paruta\"; and \"Chola/Choda\" was called \"Chora.\"\n*   There is something significant in the method of sentence formation: in inscriptions of our present-day sentence structure, subject comes first with some adjectives, then object and other words. A different sentence-writing style existed in the **Macherla inscription of Jayasimha Vallabha I** and the **Potladurthi–Malepadu inscription of Renati Cholas**.\n*   By the time of **Gunaga Vijayaditya**, Telugu had come under Sanskrit''s influence; poetry writing started with local metre, guided by matra and gana. **Taruvoja** in the inscription of Panduranga; \"seesa verses\" in the Kandukuru inscription; and \"**madhyakkara**\" in the Bezawada inscription (C.E. 930) are examples.\n*   Gradually, Telugu language attained \"**kavya**\" shape; **Pampa**, who wrote \"Vikramarjuna Vijayam\" — the first \"kavya\" in Kannada — belonged to the age of the Chalukyas. Scholars opine **Tikkana Somayaji** was the greatest not only among the poets of the Chalukyan age but among all Telugu poets — his was a scholarly family. **Bhaskara mantri** wrote \"Bhaskara Ramayananm.\" **Ketana**, who wrote \"Kadambari\" as a Telugu padya kavya, might have been Tikkana''s elder father.\n*   **Tikkana** was not only a great minister and poet — \"he was a great cultural movement.\" He wrote \"**Nirvachanottara Ramayana**,\" dedicated to Manumasiddhi. The works of **Palkuriki Somanatha** — \"Panditaradhya Charitra\" and \"Basava Puranam\" — won fame in Telugu. **Ketana**, who wrote \"Dasakumar Charitra\" and \"Vijnaneswaram,\" and **Marana**, who wrote \"Markandeya Purana,\" belonged to this age. **Narayanabhattu** helped Nannaya in writing \"**Andhra Mahabharata**.\" In this way, the Chalukyan age contributed greatly to Telugu literary development.\n\n### Art Development\n\nRoyal courts and temples of Chalukyan times were the centres for the arts of music, dance, architecture and sculpture. The early Chalukyas, at their capital Badami and its surrounding places, constructed many cave temples.\n\n*   Prominent temples came up at **Aihole and Pattadakal** in Karnataka — as many as around **70 temples at Aihole** and **10 temples at Pattadakal** were constructed, exhibiting Chalukyan architectural and sculptural styles. These temples were built by erecting a rectangular platform over which a row of pillars was arranged to bear the roof load — walls were absent on the four sides, and this structure, called \"**sandhagaram**,\" might have been used as a meeting place for village elders and people.\n*   In Mahaboobnagar district (present-day Telangana), at **Alampur** on the bank of the sacred Tungabhadra river, there are **nine temples**, otherwise called the **Navabrahma temples**. Among them, six had compound walls; the remaining stay outside this compound wall. All nine temple names end with the word \"brahma,\" but all these are actually **Siva temples**, with Siva lingas installed in them. Among these nine, \"**Viswabrahma**\" temple was the biggest; these temples still receive worship today.\n*   Temples built at **Vemulavada** belonged to the ninth and tenth centuries. Among these, **Bhimeswara temple** is important — built by **Baddega**; it was earlier called \"Baddegeswara\" temple. At its sanctum-sanctorum entrance, a **Gajalakshmi** carving was placed; the walls of the sanctum sanctorum have four pillars — a tradition of the early Chalukyas.\n*   At the **Bhimalingeswara temple** in Vemulavada, on the northern wall of the sanctum sanctorium, a sculpture of **\"Mahishasura mardini\"** was carved. **Nageswara temple** is another temple at Vemulavada, built during the time of Arikesari II — the god worshipped by merchants is called \"Nagareswara,\" with a Siva linga installed in the inner temple. A Jain temple was built at Repaka by Arikesari III (per inscriptions). In Karimnagar district, 22 km from Manthena, beside \"Adavisomnapalli\" village, **four cave temples** exist.\n*   In coastal Andhra, Eastern Chalukyas built many temples, displaying love for architecture and sculpture, showing the influence of Pallava-Chalukya traditions. **Vijayaditya II stated he built 108 temples.** Inscriptional evidence states **Gunaga Vijayaditya built the Vijayeswara temple at Bikkavolu**. **Chalukya Bhima** constructed **Bhimeswara temples at Draksharama**; the temples at **Bhimeswaram, Palakollu and Amaravati** (in Pancharamas) were also Chalukyan constructions. **Yuddhamalla** built the **Kumaraswamy temple** at Bezawada; **Mahadevi**, queen of Vikramaditya II, built a magnificent Saiva temple at Pattadakal to mark her husband''s victories. At **Yeleswaram** in Nalgonda district, many old Siva temples emerged.\n*   **Gunaga Vijayaditya** built **six temples at Bikkavolu**; among these, excepting the **Golingeswara temple**, all others are in ruined condition. **Golingeswara temple, Chandrasekhara temple and Rajarajeswara temple** were all built on a square plan, two to three storeys, with art and nagara-style vimanas — these were rebuilt in later times. **Rajarajeswara temple was built by Rajaraja Narendra.**\n*   Music and dance were patronised in royal courts and temples. **Ammaraju** held the title \"kavigayaka kalpataruvu.\" **Chalukya Bhima I** honoured **Challavva**, an adept in Gandharva Vidya, with a land donation; her father **Mallappa** was described as like Tumbura in the art of music. Sculptures of the age show musical instruments such as violin, flute, mrudangam (maddela) and talas. Names of dancers, musical-instrument players and dance teachers are found in inscriptions; several types of dance songs and different kinds of ragas (tunes) are recorded respectively in \"Panditaradhya Charitra\" and \"Kumara Sambhavam.\"\n*   Painting had great importance in Chalukyan age. Painting at **Badami** is called a beautiful specimen of Deccan painting; paintings in **cave temples two and three at Badami** (C.E. 578) are wonderful testimony to early brahmin painting. In sculptures at Vijayawada, Jammidoddi, and one found at Chebrolu, dance and instrumental \"operas\" are depicted. Among the pastimes of the period, **kolatam** — an admixture of dance and singing — was popular.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age-p13", "partName": "Chalukyan Age: Concluding Assessment", "bodyMd": "The period of Chalukyan rule could be considered a **glorious age in South Indian history**. Over their long period of rule — sixth to twelfth century — the Chalukyas achieved political unity and successfully resisted foreign invasions of South India; regarding the Arab invasion of Northern India during their time, they were able, to some extent, to prevent its spread to the western areas. Even though Chalukyan kings emerged from continuous wars, they developed South India in all fields and thereby could bring back memories of Satavahana rule.\n\n*   The rule of **Chalukya Bhima**, who ruled from Vengi as capital, deserves special mention — during his time, the Bhimeswara temple at Draksharama and one more Bhimeswara temple at Chalukya Bhimavaram were built, and the fame of the Chalukyas was taken to greater heights. During their time, though Buddhism slid back, **Jainism flourished greatly**.\n*   During the time of the **Badami Chalukyas**, Pulakesi II, as part of his military conquests, occupied most parts of Telangana and Rayalaseema and proclaimed the eminence of the Chalukyas. **Vemulavada Chalukyas**, who ruled over Telangana areas, gave the fruits of great administration to the people of Telangana — during the time of **Baddega**, who fought 42 battles without tasting defeat in any one of them, war heroes like him brought glory to the age. During the time of Vemulavada Chalukyas, **Arikesari II**, taking advantage of relations with the Rashtrakutas, was able to expand his empire. **Mudigonda Chalukyas** also, with the confidence of the Eastern Chalukyas, ruled over the Telangana region.\n*   Though local government resembled that of the Cholas, the Chalukyas divided their empire into administrative units. A large percentage of state income was spent on people''s welfare programmes, earning the people''s admiration. In army maintenance too, new changes could take place — **Pulakesi II**, in the Aihole inscription, referred to the six types of army organisations. Keeping responsibility for catching criminals with local officials, and giving importance to village assemblies in judicial trials, remained useful and praiseworthy measures.\n*   Giving importance to tank construction contributed to improving crop yields — the *' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '*Chaita tank** and **Bhima Samudram**, mentioned in the Tadikonda inscription of Ammaraju II, still supply water to cultivated lands today. Along with agriculture, industries, trade and commerce proceeded on a progressive path; weaving of different kinds of cloth gained importance among the handicrafts.\n*   Though the four-fold varna system was prevalent, **sudras and women could freely take up their activities and enjoy their rights**. Saiva and Jain faiths enjoyed good patronage and progress — reflected in temples constructed at **Alampuram, Vemulavada, Kolanupaka, Aihole and Pattadakal**, which stand even today as specimens of Chalukyan architectural and sculptural style.\n*   Next to Saivism and Jainism, Vaishnavism also gained importance and enjoyed patronage. In literary field, inscriptions and writings began in Prakrit and Sanskrit languages, and gradually — during the time of the Eastern Chalukyas and Vemulavada Chalukyas — **Telugu, the language of the local people, started becoming the medium for inscriptions and writings**; with the preparation of \"Mahabharata\" in Telugu by **Nannaya**, court poet of Rajaraja Narendra, the way became clear for the development of Telugu language and literature.\n\nIt could be realised that the Chalukyas, during their long rule of six centuries, could introduce **stable, praiseworthy, popular, ideal and trend-setting systems and changes** in political, administrative, economic, social and cultural fields. In that way, their rule period could be considered as an **age by itself** in Telangana as well as South Indian history, without any doubt.", "free": false}]' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter5-chalukyan-age';
